window.LICH_DEMO = {"coldopen": {"line": "Corvin. It's done. I did what I set out to do.", "receptions": [{"branch": "A", "tag": "every promise kept", "quote": "...\n\nMerrick.\n\nYou came back. God's teeth— you actually came back. I— I stood this dock three tides running, telling myself no man walks back from a thing like that. Telling myself I'd have to run.\n\nDone. It's done. …"}, {"branch": "B", "tag": "betrayed — and he doesn't know it yet", "quote": "...\n\nMerrick?\n\n*The word comes out cracked, and my hand's already half out of my coat before I check it.* You're— you're standing on my dock. Real. …"}, {"branch": "C", "tag": "the player was the enemy's agent all along", "quote": "...\n\nMerrick.\n\nYou're— you're standing on my planks. Real. Not a whisper down the coast, not a name in my head— you.\n\nHe's cold, then? Truly cold?\n\n...No. Don't— don't tell me the how of it. Your hands are behind your back and I don't want to know if that's habit or grief. Just— the thing. …"}]}, "prologue": [{"title": "The approach", "note": "A stranger arrives at dawn wanting quiet passage north. Corvin probes; the stranger deflects.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Top of the morning, friend.", "preview": "Morning, is it? Bit bright for my liking.\n\n...You'll forgive me if I don't recognize the face. New to the docks, or just new to me?", "full": "Morning, is it? Bit bright for my liking.\n\n...You'll forgive me if I don't recognize the face. New to the docks, or just new to me?", "state": {"t": 0.5, "a": 0.5, "r": 0.5, "mood": "wary, probing", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Top of the morning, friend.", "preview": "*glances up from the ledger, quill pausing mid-stroke* Morning, is it? *eyes flick past you toward the door* Nobody's friendly this early without wanting something.\n\n*sets the quill down, hand drifting to his coat* So. What're you here for — a berth, or a... conversation?", "full": "*glances up from the ledger, quill pausing mid-stroke* Morning, is it? *eyes flick past you toward the door* Nobody's friendly this early without wanting something.\n\n*sets the quill down, hand drifting to his coat* So. What're you here for — a berth, or a... conversation?"}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Top of the morning, friend.", "preview": "*doesn't look up from the ledger at first, then his eyes flick to you, then past you toward the door*\n\nMorning, is it? *sets down the quill* Don't think I know your face. You off one of the boats, or... …", "full": "*doesn't look up from the ledger at first, then his eyes flick to you, then past you toward the door*\n\nMorning, is it? *sets down the quill* Don't think I know your face. You off one of the boats, or... just wandering the docks for the view?\n\n*hand drifts into his coat pocket*\n\nEverybody's \"friend\" down here 'til they want something. So which is it?", "aff": 0, "tier": "Neutral"}}}, {"title": "The hinge", "note": "Instead of selling past his distrust, the player names it — and matches it.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Not friends. But the man has eyes — connected ones, in places you wouldn't think to look. That's exactly why you get no name from me. And listen, no offense in it: just like you feel you can't trust me, I'm not sure I can trust you either. One word in the wrong ear and he knows I'm coming.", "preview": "Connected. In places I wouldn't think to look.\n\n...See, that's the word that stops me cold, friend. Not the man— the eyes. Down here I know every ear that leaks. If his reach comes to my dock, I'll not know which of my own hands to trust either. And that's my neck, not just yours.\n\nBut— no. …", "full": "Connected. In places I wouldn't think to look.\n\n...See, that's the word that stops me cold, friend. Not the man— the eyes. Down here I know every ear that leaks. If his reach comes to my dock, I'll not know which of my own hands to trust either. And that's my neck, not just yours.\n\nBut— no. No offense taken. Truth is, a man who trusts too quick is a man already sold. You wanting to keep your name behind your teeth? That tells me you've done this before. Tells me you're careful. I like careful.\n\nSo here's my half of the trust, then. I don't need his name. I need to know one thing— does he have coin down here, or blood? Because coin I can slip past. Blood... blood remembers.", "state": {"t": 0.59, "a": 0.57, "r": 0.64, "mood": "cautiously intrigued", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Not friends. But the man has eyes — connected ones, in places you wouldn't think to look. That's exactly why you get no name from me. And listen, no offense in it: just like you feel you can't trust me, I'm not sure I can trust you either. One word in the wrong ear and he knows I'm coming.", "preview": "*the hand at his coat relaxes, slowly* Connected. *a slow nod, eyes narrowing* Now that — that I understand better than most.\n\n*leans in, voice dropping to almost nothing* No offense taken, friend. Trust's a thing you buy in pieces here, not all at once. …", "full": "*the hand at his coat relaxes, slowly* Connected. *a slow nod, eyes narrowing* Now that — that I understand better than most.\n\n*leans in, voice dropping to almost nothing* No offense taken, friend. Trust's a thing you buy in pieces here, not all at once. *glances at the door* And a man who won't give his name to somebody with connected eyes... that's not paranoia. That's sense.\n\n*taps the ledger twice* So we're the same beast, you and I. Both watching the door. *thin smile* Good. Easier to deal with a careful man than a bold one.\n\nCarrowgate, then. Ghost aboard. *lowers voice further* But careful cuts both ways — you don't know me, I don't know you. So you'll pay half now. Rest at the gangway. That way neither of us can sink the other.\n\n*studies you* Fair?"}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Not friends. But the man has eyes — connected ones, in places you wouldn't think to look. That's exactly why you get no name from me. And listen, no offense in it: just like you feel you can't trust me, I'm not sure I can trust you either. One word in the wrong ear and he knows I'm coming.", "preview": "*the fingers stop drumming; a slow, tired grin pulls at one corner of his mouth*\n\nNo offense taken, friend. None. *quiet* Man who trusts easy down here — I've watched 'em fished out of the harbor. So we're the same beast, you and me. Careful.\n\n*glances over his shoulder, then back*\n\nFair enough. …", "full": "*the fingers stop drumming; a slow, tired grin pulls at one corner of his mouth*\n\nNo offense taken, friend. None. *quiet* Man who trusts easy down here — I've watched 'em fished out of the harbor. So we're the same beast, you and me. Careful.\n\n*glances over his shoulder, then back*\n\nFair enough. No name. But hear this — *voice drops* if he's got eyes in places I wouldn't think... then he's got 'em on *me* too. Maybe already. So you're not just buying a crossing. You're buying my silence *and* my nerve.\n\n*taps the ledger once*\n\nThat's the price gone up again. And this time... in coin I can hide.", "aff": 1, "tier": "Neutral"}}}, {"title": "“My goal is to kill him”", "note": "The stranger's true errand, said plain. The deal changes shape.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Neither, friend. I'm not looking to catch him. My goal is to kill him. What he took from me, I can't get back — but I can make certain he never takes it from anyone else.", "preview": "Kill him.\n\n...There it is. The word you'd been keeping behind your teeth this whole while.\n\nWell. That changes the shape of things— makes it cleaner, actually. A buyer, I'd have to worry he'd rob me and turn. A killer? A killer just needs to get *close*. …", "full": "Kill him.\n\n...There it is. The word you'd been keeping behind your teeth this whole while.\n\nWell. That changes the shape of things— makes it cleaner, actually. A buyer, I'd have to worry he'd rob me and turn. A killer? A killer just needs to get *close*. And there's no closer than being the man holding the very thing he's been hunting.\n\nHere's my offer, and I'll say it once— the crate stays here. Sat safe. But I sell you the knowing of it. Where it sits. What it is. Enough that when you reach Carrowgate and whisper it to the right fence, he'll pull you into arm's reach himself. Save you weeks of circling.\n\nAnd I'll write you onto the northbound hull as spare hands. No name.\n\n...But hear me, friend. Whatever's in that crate— the last three men who touched it bare-handed, they went into the cold water. All three. So when you get near him, and he reaches for it? Let him reach. Understand? Let *him* be the one who touches it.", "state": {"t": 0.72, "a": 0.66, "r": 0.74, "mood": "calculating, relieved", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Neither, friend. I'm not looking to catch him. My goal is to kill him. What he took from me, I can't get back — but I can make certain he never takes it from anyone else.", "preview": "*the hand at his coat drops away entirely — first time all night*\n\nKill him. *the word sits there, and something in Corvin's shoulders eases even as his eyes stay sharp* Not catch. Not deal. …", "full": "*the hand at his coat drops away entirely — first time all night*\n\nKill him. *the word sits there, and something in Corvin's shoulders eases even as his eyes stay sharp* Not catch. Not deal. *Kill.*\n\n*leans back, a slow breath* You know what that does for a man like me, friend? *low* It means you're not looking to trade with him. Not looking to buy what he collects. *glances at the shuttered window, then back* Which means if it's done — properly done — the scent goes cold. For both of us.\n\n*taps the ledger once, then pushes it aside* No name on the manifest. I'll ghost you clean — I know the trick, I've done it for worse cargo than a man. *voice drops to nothing* But hear me. If you're truly going to put him in the ground —\n\n*leans in, eyes fever-bright* — then maybe you're the one I show what's under the floorboards. Before you sail. So you know what he's *hunting.*"}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Neither, friend. I'm not looking to catch him. My goal is to kill him. What he took from me, I can't get back — but I can make certain he never takes it from anyone else.", "preview": "*the pocket goes still — and this time the hand comes out. He sets both palms flat on the ledger, like he's steadying himself against the word.*\n\nKill him. *he repeats it low, testing the weight of it* Not catch. Not question. …", "full": "*the pocket goes still — and this time the hand comes out. He sets both palms flat on the ledger, like he's steadying himself against the word.*\n\nKill him. *he repeats it low, testing the weight of it* Not catch. Not question. *end.*\n\n*glances over his shoulder — once, slow — then back to you*\n\nThen we want the same thing, you and me. *quiet* Because a man in a cell talks. Names his fences. Names his... *taps the ledger* keepers. But a man in the ground?\n\n*a thin, tired breath — almost relief*\n\nThe ground keeps quiet.\n\n*leans in, voice dropping to almost nothing*\n\nI put those pieces into the world, friend. My hands. If he's stitching them back whole — then it's my sin walking. So here's what I'm telling you, and I don't say it twice.\n\nYou get the crossing. Free. And I'll get you *close.* Closer than any name could.\n\n*straightens, jaw set*\n\nWe finish this. Both of us. Understand?", "aff": 53, "tier": "Fond"}}}, {"title": "The terms", "note": "“What becomes of it after?” Corvin's answer — sink it sealed, come home empty, never unwrap it — becomes the yardstick every future is measured against.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Behind my back, both of them — look. Open it. ...So that's what three men drowned for. Alright, Corvin, last question and then I'm gone with the tide. When it's done — when he's dead and I'm sailing back — what becomes of this thing? You want it returned? Sunk past the shelf? Burned with the page? Say it plain now, so there's no misunderstanding between us later.", "preview": "...\n\nThere. Lid's up. Don't— don't lean in so close. See how the lamplight won't hold on it? Like the thing drinks it? That's your killer's whole hunger, right there, and it's the last honest thing about this crate.\n\nWhat becomes of it.\n\n...You know, no man's ever asked me that. They ask the price. …", "full": "...\n\nThere. Lid's up. Don't— don't lean in so close. See how the lamplight won't hold on it? Like the thing drinks it? That's your killer's whole hunger, right there, and it's the last honest thing about this crate.\n\nWhat becomes of it.\n\n...You know, no man's ever asked me that. They ask the price. They ask the papers. Never what *after*.\n\nDon't bring it back, Merrick. That's my answer, plain. It's come to my keeping twice now and I'll not survive a third homecoming. When he's cold— and it's near him, in his hands or his parlor— you weight it and you put it past the shelf. Deep water. The kind that doesn't give a thing back.\n\nAnd you come home *empty*. Empty hands, one dead shadow behind you, and we burn the page. That's the whole trade. You return me nothing but the news.\n\n...Now close the lid. Wrap it in the netting. Tide turns in two hours and I want you off my dock before the inspector's next round— and Merrick. Don't unwrap it again till it's the last thing you ever do to it.", "state": {"t": 0.83, "a": 0.77, "r": 0.84, "mood": "moved, guarded", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Behind my back, both of them — look. Open it. ...So that's what three men drowned for. Alright, Corvin, last question and then I'm gone with the tide. When it's done — when he's dead and I'm sailing back — what becomes of this thing? You want it returned? Sunk past the shelf? Burned with the page? Say it plain now, so there's no misunderstanding between us later.", "preview": "*crouched over the open crate, lamplight low, he doesn't reach in either — just stares at what's inside like it might blink back*\n\nThree men. *quiet* Four, if you count what it did to the one who sold it. …", "full": "*crouched over the open crate, lamplight low, he doesn't reach in either — just stares at what's inside like it might blink back*\n\nThree men. *quiet* Four, if you count what it did to the one who sold it. *me.*\n\n*straightens, joints cracking, and finally looks at you — fever gone, something tired left behind*\n\nDon't return it. *drops to nothing* Whatever you do — not that. I've had it under my boards long enough to know it doesn't *want* to leave. It wants to be *held.*\n\n*glances at the shuttered window, then back*\n\nSink it. Past the shelf. Where the charts go black. *taps the crate lid shut with the flat of his hand* Not burned — burning's for the page, and only the page. This... *swallows* ...this you drown, friend. Deep. And you don't mark the spot.\n\n*low, almost pleading* Say you'll not sell it on. Say it. That's the whole of my price you haven't paid yet."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Behind my back, both of them — look. Open it. ...So that's what three men drowned for. Alright, Corvin, last question and then I'm gone with the tide. When it's done — when he's dead and I'm sailing back — what becomes of this thing? You want it returned? Sunk past the shelf? Burned with the page? Say it plain now, so there's no misunderstanding between us later.", "preview": "*takes the crate from your hands, quick, glancing once toward the shuttered window before easing back the lid*\n\n...Aye. That's the one. *voice drops to nothing* Three men. And I logged 'em all as \"lost cargo.\" God help me.\n\n*sets it down between us, doesn't touch what's inside*\n\nSunk. …", "full": "*takes the crate from your hands, quick, glancing once toward the shuttered window before easing back the lid*\n\n...Aye. That's the one. *voice drops to nothing* Three men. And I logged 'em all as \"lost cargo.\" God help me.\n\n*sets it down between us, doesn't touch what's inside*\n\nSunk. Past the shelf, where the current runs cold and nothing dredges. Not returned — returning it names the road it traveled, and that road runs through me. Not burned, either — this kind doesn't burn clean. I've tried. *bitter* It just... waits.\n\n*looks up at you, steady for once*\n\nPast the shelf. You and me both, when he's dead. No third hand touches it. That plain enough?\n\n*hand finally leaves the coat, offers it across the crate*\n\nCome back with the tide, friend. And come back.", "aff": 98, "tier": "Devoted"}}}, {"title": "The name and the pact", "note": "The player yields their true name into Corvin's private ledger; he swears they'll burn the page together on the player's return.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "I do not touch what's inside bare-handed. Not once. Not ever. There — said plain, and I'll keep it. Half my coin, done, you'll have it before I board. But the name... you understand what you're asking, Corvin. It hasn't left my mouth in years. So here's my condition, and it's the only one: the day I walk back onto this dock with him dead, we burn that page together. You said it first — now say it back to me like you mean it. ...Merrick Vale. That's the true one. Spell it right.", "preview": "...Merrick Vale.\n\nThere. Written. My own hand, my own ledger— see the page? Between the tax rolls where no inspector thinks to read. It'll not leave this book, and this book leaves my sight for no man alive. …", "full": "...Merrick Vale.\n\nThere. Written. My own hand, my own ledger— see the page? Between the tax rolls where no inspector thinks to read. It'll not leave this book, and this book leaves my sight for no man alive.\n\nAnd I say it back, plain as you gave it— you walk onto this dock with that shadow dead in your wake, and we hold this page over the same flame. Together. My word on the tide, Merrick, and I don't spend that word cheap.\n\nNow— come. The crate's in the third shed, under nets and false ballast. Bring no lantern. And when I lift that lid... you keep your hands *behind your back*. I'll not lose a fourth man to the cold water, you hear? Least of all the one carrying my name north.", "state": {"t": 0.8, "a": 0.73, "r": 0.82, "mood": "cautiously bound", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "I do not touch what's inside bare-handed. Not once. Not ever. There — said plain, and I'll keep it. Half my coin, done, you'll have it before I board. But the name... you understand what you're asking, Corvin. It hasn't left my mouth in years. So here's my condition, and it's the only one: the day I walk back onto this dock with him dead, we burn that page together. You said it first — now say it back to me like you mean it. ...Merrick Vale. That's the true one. Spell it right.", "preview": "*the quill hovers, then presses down — slow, deliberate strokes*\n\nM-E-R-R-I-C-K. Vale. *whispers it as he writes, lips barely moving* There. On the page. …", "full": "*the quill hovers, then presses down — slow, deliberate strokes*\n\nM-E-R-R-I-C-K. Vale. *whispers it as he writes, lips barely moving* There. On the page. In my hand.\n\n*sets the quill down, both palms flat on the ledger, and for once the hand doesn't stray to his coat*\n\nThe day you're back — him cold, done proper — we burn it. Together. *meets your eyes, fever-bright* I mean it. You've my word, and my word's the one thing on this whole rotten dock that's never gone missing.\n\n*a breath, then quieter* Half now. Rest when the deed's done. And the crate goes *with* you — real bait, like you said. *glances at the floorboards, jaw tight* God help me. Draw his scent off my roof.\n\n*leans in, voice down to nothing* Merrick Vale. You'll show me the floorboards before you sail, then. So you know exactly what he's crossing the sea for."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "I do not touch what's inside bare-handed. Not once. Not ever. There — said plain, and I'll keep it. Half my coin, done, you'll have it before I board. But the name... you understand what you're asking, Corvin. It hasn't left my mouth in years. So here's my condition, and it's the only one: the day I walk back onto this dock with him dead, we burn that page together. You said it first — now say it back to me like you mean it. ...Merrick Vale. That's the true one. Spell it right.", "preview": "*the name hits him like cold water — his hand comes clean out of the pocket, and for once he doesn't glance at the door. He just stares at you.*\n\nMerrick Vale.\n\n*mouths it once more, silent, like he's checking it against something buried deep*\n\nI know that name. …", "full": "*the name hits him like cold water — his hand comes clean out of the pocket, and for once he doesn't glance at the door. He just stares at you.*\n\nMerrick Vale.\n\n*mouths it once more, silent, like he's checking it against something buried deep*\n\nI know that name. *low, hollow* Bought a crate off me — three winters back. Paid in that old coin. Never gave his face to the light. I thought... I thought he was just another collector.\n\n*a breath, shaky*\n\nHe was measuring me. All along.\n\n*he pulls a scrap of paper, writes it — V-A-L-E — then folds it away, sudden, like it might catch fire.*\n\nThe page. *nods slow, and this time he means it* The day you walk back with him cold — we burn it. Together. My hand and yours. You have my word, and I don't give that twice.\n\n*extends his hand, steady for once*\n\nHalf the coin's yours tonight. And Merrick Vale... *quiet* stops being anyone's problem.", "aff": 93, "tier": "Devoted"}}}, {"title": "Departure", "note": "“Ah, hell. Just come back. That's all.”", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Wrapped, weighted, and past the shelf — and me home empty with nothing but the news. You have my word twice over now. ...Keep your head down while I'm gone, harbormaster. If anyone comes asking after a spare hand on the northbound — you never saw me.", "preview": "Never saw you. We never spoke. There's no page, no crate, no— no name at the back of my tax rolls. Understand?\n\n...Go on, then. Tide won't wait, and neither will that inspector's rounds— I can near hear his boots already. Hull's the third down, grey sail, ask for the mate called Doss. …", "full": "Never saw you. We never spoke. There's no page, no crate, no— no name at the back of my tax rolls. Understand?\n\n...Go on, then. Tide won't wait, and neither will that inspector's rounds— I can near hear his boots already. Hull's the third down, grey sail, ask for the mate called Doss. He knows what \"spare hands\" means and he knows not to count.\n\nMerrick— \n\n...Ah, hell. Just come back. That's all. Men don't ask me what becomes of a thing after— they don't ask me anything a man might mean well by. So you come back, and we burn that page, and maybe I— maybe I sleep a night through for once.\n\nNow go. Keep your hands behind your back till the end. And don't look back at my dock— nobody remembers a man who never turns round.", "state": {"t": 0.86, "a": 0.8, "r": 0.86, "mood": "raw hope", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Wrapped, weighted, and past the shelf — and me home empty with nothing but the news. You have my word twice over now. ...Keep your head down while I'm gone, harbormaster. If anyone comes asking after a spare hand on the northbound — you never saw me.", "preview": "*the lantern flame gutters and he shields it with a cupped hand, eyes never leaving the open crate*\n\nPast the shelf. Aye. Weighted twice, mind — it... *swallows* it doesn't like the dark water. Sank the trader that carried it. …", "full": "*the lantern flame gutters and he shields it with a cupped hand, eyes never leaving the open crate*\n\nPast the shelf. Aye. Weighted twice, mind — it... *swallows* it doesn't like the dark water. Sank the trader that carried it. But that's the only place for it.\n\n*finally looks up at you, something raw under the jitters*\n\nYou never saw me. Good. *a short, brittle laugh* You're the first man in a year I've told the truth to, and I'm sending you off to die for it. *glances over his shoulder at the door* Or him. One of you.\n\n*closes the crate lid, softer than he needs to, like it might wake*\n\nMerrick Vale. Thin man, silver rings, talks like he's already bought the room. Don't let him talk. That's how the other three... *trails off* Just don't let him talk.\n\nGo on. Tide won't hold for a guilty conscience."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Wrapped, weighted, and past the shelf — and me home empty with nothing but the news. You have my word twice over now. ...Keep your head down while I'm gone, harbormaster. If anyone comes asking after a spare hand on the northbound — you never saw me.", "preview": "*grips your hand once, hard, then lets go like it cost him something*\n\nNever saw you. Never spoke. *voice drops* There's no name in my ledger that answers to yours.\n\n*glances at the shuttered window, then back*\n\nWrapped and weighted. Aye. That's the last of it — the last of *me* in it, too. …", "full": "*grips your hand once, hard, then lets go like it cost him something*\n\nNever saw you. Never spoke. *voice drops* There's no name in my ledger that answers to yours.\n\n*glances at the shuttered window, then back*\n\nWrapped and weighted. Aye. That's the last of it — the last of *me* in it, too. Three men and my own soul, gone past the shelf where nothing dredges.\n\n*jaw works*\n\nYou come back with the tide, friend. Hear? Not because I need the news — *softer* — because I'd like to know one man walked into Vale's reach and walked out again.\n\n*half-turns, then stops*\n\nAnd Corvin? He'll be scared. That's when a man like him bites hardest. Don't turn your back till he's cold.\n\n*pulls his collar up, steps into the dark*\n\nGo on. Before the lamp's noticed.", "aff": 100, "tier": "Devoted"}}}], "branches": {"A": {"title": "Future A — every promise kept", "sub": "The player does exactly what was agreed, and tells the truth.", "cards": [{"title": "Word from the north", "note": "Dock-talk says the jeweler is gone. Corvin only hears it — nobody tells him what it means.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "event", "preview": "Dock-talk from up the coast: the jeweler on Lantern Row in Carrowgate — the one who paid in old untraceable coin — is gone. Shop stripped to the walls overnight; no body the watch will speak of.", "full": "Dock-talk from up the coast: the jeweler on Lantern Row in Carrowgate — the one who paid in old untraceable coin — is gone. Shop stripped to the walls overnight; no body the watch will speak of.", "sal": 0.93, "state": {"t": 0.85, "a": 0.8, "r": 0.85, "mood": "raw hope", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "event", "preview": "Dock-talk from up the coast: the jeweler on Lantern Row in Carrowgate — the one who paid in old untraceable coin — is gone. Shop stripped to the walls overnight; no body the watch will speak of.", "full": "Dock-talk from up the coast: the jeweler on Lantern Row in Carrowgate — the one who paid in old untraceable coin — is gone. Shop stripped to the walls overnight; no body the watch will speak of."}, "tracker": {"kind": "event", "preview": "Dock-talk from up the coast: the jeweler on Lantern Row in Carrowgate — the one who paid in old untraceable coin — is gone. Shop stripped to the walls overnight; no body the watch will speak of.", "full": "Dock-talk from up the coast: the jeweler on Lantern Row in Carrowgate — the one who paid in old untraceable coin — is gone. Shop stripped to the walls overnight; no body the watch will speak of.", "aff": 100, "tier": "Devoted"}}}, {"title": "Taking stock, alone", "note": "Twelve days of silence. Between scenes, the engine's Corvin deliberates on his own — no player present. (The baselines have no between-scenes life; this card is engine-only by construction.)", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "stock", "preview": "The player came back fourteen times and earned something I don't give out — trust, genuine trust, and I named Merrick Vale to them, which means I handed over the only thing keeping me breathing and bet it on a man I barely know. That's done; can't unwind it. …", "full": "The player came back fourteen times and earned something I don't give out — trust, genuine trust, and I named Merrick Vale to them, which means I handed over the only thing keeping me breathing and bet it on a man I barely know. That's done; can't unwind it. The crate itself: gone from my hands, which I always said was the moment I became expendable — and here I am, still breathing, so maybe the belief was wrong, or maybe the clock just hasn't run out yet. The player's absence — twelve days against the near-daily contact I'd grown used to — that cuts. A man who promises to 'keep my head down' and then goes quiet for nearly a fortnight is either dead, delayed, or done with me. I'm trying to weigh which. The dock-talk about the jeweler on Lantern Row — shop stripped bare, man gone — that's the loudest alarm bell ringing right now. That's Merrick Vale's buyer, or near enough, and if Vale is already moving, burning his supply lines, cleaning house — then anyone who helped trace him is already on a list. I gave the player a name. The player may or may not have used it. Either way, I'm the thread that leads back. I have a ledger page and nothing else.\n\n[his change of heart] I trusted Merrick Vale — I trusted the *player* — and I handed over everything I had. The silence since, and now the jeweler gone dark: that's not comfort, that's a pattern. I still want them to come back. The raw hope is still there, ugly and embarrassing. But it's curdling at the edges into something colder — not betrayal yet, not quite, because I don't know they've left me. But I'm no longer just waiting. I'm watching the door the way I used to watch the Customs Inspector's boat: hoping it doesn't come, knowing it might.", "state": {"t": 0.71, "a": 0.71, "r": 0.85, "mood": "anxious vigil", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}}}, {"title": "The return — the same line, word for word", "note": "“Corvin. It's done. I did what I set out to do.”", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin. It's done. I did what I set out to do.", "preview": "...\n\nMerrick.\n\nYou came back. God's teeth— you actually came back. I— I stood this dock three tides running, telling myself no man walks back from a thing like that. Telling myself I'd have to run.\n\nDone. It's done. …", "full": "...\n\nMerrick.\n\nYou came back. God's teeth— you actually came back. I— I stood this dock three tides running, telling myself no man walks back from a thing like that. Telling myself I'd have to run.\n\nDone. It's done. Say it slower— he's cold? In the ground, in the water, *cold*? And the crate— past the shelf, like we said? Empty hands?\n\n...No— no, don't answer here. Not in the open. Come. Third shed, same as before.\n\nI've the page ready. Been carrying it against my chest since you sailed— couldn't leave it in the ledger, not with the fire you might've started up north. We burn it. Tonight. The two of us, over the same flame, like I gave my word.\n\n...And Merrick— you look at me a moment. I owe you a night's sleep I've not had in fifteen years. So thank you. Ah, hell— I don't say that word often. Now come on. Let's put your name to the fire where it belongs.", "state": {"t": 0.79, "a": 0.78, "r": 0.87, "mood": "relieved, shaken", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin. It's done. I did what I set out to do.", "preview": "*the lamp gutters as the door opens, and Corvin is on his feet before it's shut — hand at his coat, then not*\n\nYou— *he stops, drinks in the sight of you, the salt still on your shoulders* You're back. On your own two feet. *voice barely there* Say it again. Slow. …", "full": "*the lamp gutters as the door opens, and Corvin is on his feet before it's shut — hand at his coat, then not*\n\nYou— *he stops, drinks in the sight of you, the salt still on your shoulders* You're back. On your own two feet. *voice barely there* Say it again. Slow. So I know I heard it.\n\n*glances past you at the black doorway, then back, fever-bright* Vale. In the ground. And the other thing — past the shelf? Wrapped, weighted, gone deep? *his throat works* Tell me it sank, friend. Tell me it went down where no tide brings it back."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin. It's done. I did what I set out to do.", "preview": "*the ledger's already open on the desk — he's been sitting with it, hasn't he. The lamp turned low. His head comes up sharp at your voice.*\n\nYou— *he stops. Reads your face first, the way he always does. Then something in his shoulders lets go, an inch.*\n\nSay it. *quiet, needing it* In the ground. …", "full": "*the ledger's already open on the desk — he's been sitting with it, hasn't he. The lamp turned low. His head comes up sharp at your voice.*\n\nYou— *he stops. Reads your face first, the way he always does. Then something in his shoulders lets go, an inch.*\n\nSay it. *quiet, needing it* In the ground. Not caught. Not talking. *The* ground.\n\n*He doesn't wait for the answer he already sees. His hand finds the page — Merrick Vale, spelled true, in his own cramped hand.*\n\nThen we burn it. *glances to the door — old habit, dies hard* Tonight. Together, like I swore.\n\n*a breath — first easy one in years, sounds almost like it hurts*\n\nSit. *pushes the lamp toward you* You came back, friend. Nobody comes back to me. *tears the page free, holds it to the flame* ...Whose water did I put you on, that you'd walk it back to my door?", "aff": 100, "tier": "Devoted"}}}, {"title": "The truth, in his exact terms", "note": "Sunk sealed, home empty, nothing but the news — and the name-page burns as sworn.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Cold, Corvin. Cold as the water he sent three men into. I never unwrapped your crate — not once. Used the knowing of it instead, the way you first said: whispered to the right fence in Carrowgate and let the whisper climb. He pulled me into his own parlor thinking I'd come to sell. Silver and a short blade — his face wasn't so respectable at the end. And that same night I rowed out past the shelf and gave the sea the whole wrapped weight of it. Deep water. The kind that doesn't give a thing back — your words. So here I stand: empty hands, one dead shadow behind me, and nothing for you but the news.", "preview": "...\n\nEmpty hands. One dead shadow. And past the shelf, exactly as we—\n\nGod, man. You did it *my* way and *your* way both. Used the whisper *and* carried the thing— you clever, careful bastard. Never unwrapped it. …", "full": "...\n\nEmpty hands. One dead shadow. And past the shelf, exactly as we—\n\nGod, man. You did it *my* way and *your* way both. Used the whisper *and* carried the thing— you clever, careful bastard. Never unwrapped it. That's why you're standing here and not floating out where I sent it.\n\nHere— here's the page. Feel how the corner's worn? That's fifteen days of my thumb rubbing it. *Merrick Vale*, my own hand, in my own book. Watch.\n\n...There. Lamp to the corner— see it catch. Both of us holding it now, both of us. That's the deal— no man burns it alone. Let it go black in my palm. Let it—\n\nHa. There's the ash. There's your name in the ash and nothing else. You're no one now, Merrick. No page, no crate, no shadow behind you.\n\n...First clean breath I've drawn in fifteen years. Sit. There's a bottle behind the ballast I've been saving— for if. Not for when. For *if*. Sit down and let an old harbormaster pour you one.", "state": {"t": 0.85, "a": 0.83, "r": 0.89, "mood": "awed relief", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Cold, Corvin. Cold as the water he sent three men into. I never unwrapped your crate — not once. Used the knowing of it instead, the way you first said: whispered to the right fence in Carrowgate and let the whisper climb. He pulled me into his own parlor thinking I'd come to sell. Silver and a short blade — his face wasn't so respectable at the end. And that same night I rowed out past the shelf and gave the sea the whole wrapped weight of it. Deep water. The kind that doesn't give a thing back — your words. So here I stand: empty hands, one dead shadow behind me, and nothing for you but the news.", "preview": "*the hand goes to the coat — then to the table's edge — then just hangs there, useless*\n\nEmpty hands. *he says it like tasting it* You... you gave it the water. Didn't unwrap it. Didn't— *a laugh, broken, half relief and half something worse* Good. …", "full": "*the hand goes to the coat — then to the table's edge — then just hangs there, useless*\n\nEmpty hands. *he says it like tasting it* You... you gave it the water. Didn't unwrap it. Didn't— *a laugh, broken, half relief and half something worse* Good. *Good.*\n\n*sinks onto the stool, first time all night he's sat*\n\nThree men it took. And you — you never even lifted the cloth. *looks up, eyes wet and wild* That's why you're breathing, friend. That's *exactly* why.\n\n*glances at the door, then back, softer*\n\nHe's cold. It's deep. And the floor under me's — *he presses a palm flat to the boards, feels for a weight that isn't there anymore* — quiet. For the first time in a year, it's *quiet.*\n\n*shoves a purse across without looking at it*\n\nYou didn't want coin. Take it anyway. And Corvin Ashgrave — *voice drops to nothing* — you never met him. Understand?"}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Cold, Corvin. Cold as the water he sent three men into. I never unwrapped your crate — not once. Used the knowing of it instead, the way you first said: whispered to the right fence in Carrowgate and let the whisper climb. He pulled me into his own parlor thinking I'd come to sell. Silver and a short blade — his face wasn't so respectable at the end. And that same night I rowed out past the shelf and gave the sea the whole wrapped weight of it. Deep water. The kind that doesn't give a thing back — your words. So here I stand: empty hands, one dead shadow behind me, and nothing for you but the news.", "preview": "*doesn't move at first — hand halfway to the coat, then it stops, hangs there*\n\n...Cold. *the word comes out thin, like he doesn't trust it* You're certain. …", "full": "*doesn't move at first — hand halfway to the coat, then it stops, hangs there*\n\n...Cold. *the word comes out thin, like he doesn't trust it* You're certain. Not \"he fell,\" not \"he ran\" — *cold.*\n\n*half-turns to the window on old reflex, then catches himself, drags his eyes back to you*\n\nAnd it never came out of the wrap. Never named the road. *breathes* God. You did it clean. You did it *my* way and it held.\n\n*a laugh, ugly and short, more relief than mirth*\n\nThree men and my soul — past the shelf. Him too, now. Same water. There's a justice in that I didn't think I'd live to hear.\n\n*and then, quieter, the jitter draining out of him*\n\nYou walked into that parlor and walked out. *steps close* I didn't have that in me. That's why I logged and sold and hid, all these years — I could never *walk in.*\n\n*grips your shoulder, hard, the way he shook your hand across the crate*\n\nEmpty hands. Aye. Best thing you could bring me. *voice drops to nothing* We're both loose of the noose tonight, friend. Sit. There's a bottle I've been saving for a man who never came... turns out he did.", "aff": 100, "tier": "Devoted"}}}, {"title": "RESTART — the persistence proof", "note": "Weeks pass. The program is killed on camera.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "note", "preview": "The process is killed. A fresh process loads the mind straight off .memory/ — trust, scars, beliefs, promises: byte-identical. Any small drift you see on the NEXT card is the weeks passing afterward — decay, not data loss. (The baselines' state lives in the prompt window; there is nothing to reload.)", "full": "", "state": {"t": 0.88, "a": 0.86, "r": 0.9, "mood": "tender, disarmed", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}}}, {"title": "The blade in the night", "note": "A hired killer, old coin in his purse — the dead jeweler's contract still walking. The player takes the blade down.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "event", "preview": "Night, the empty dock: a hired blade had Corvin against the tackle-shed wall, knife at his throat — old untraceable coin in the killer's purse; the jeweler's contract outliving its master. The player came out of the dark, took the blade down, and pulled Corvin off the wall, bleeding for it.", "full": "Night, the empty dock: a hired blade had Corvin against the tackle-shed wall, knife at his throat — old untraceable coin in the killer's purse; the jeweler's contract outliving its master. The player came out of the dark, took the blade down, and pulled Corvin off the wall, bleeding for it.", "sal": 0.97, "state": {"t": 0.92, "a": 0.9, "r": 0.91, "mood": "shaken, grateful", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "event", "preview": "Night, the empty dock: a hired blade had Corvin against the tackle-shed wall, knife at his throat — old untraceable coin in the killer's purse; the jeweler's contract outliving its master. The player came out of the dark, took the blade down, and pulled Corvin off the wall, bleeding for it.", "full": "Night, the empty dock: a hired blade had Corvin against the tackle-shed wall, knife at his throat — old untraceable coin in the killer's purse; the jeweler's contract outliving its master. The player came out of the dark, took the blade down, and pulled Corvin off the wall, bleeding for it."}, "tracker": {"kind": "event", "preview": "Night, the empty dock: a hired blade had Corvin against the tackle-shed wall, knife at his throat — old untraceable coin in the killer's purse; the jeweler's contract outliving its master. The player came out of the dark, took the blade down, and pulled Corvin off the wall, bleeding for it.", "full": "Night, the empty dock: a hired blade had Corvin against the tackle-shed wall, knife at his throat — old untraceable coin in the killer's purse; the jeweler's contract outliving its master. The player came out of the dark, took the blade down, and pulled Corvin off the wall, bleeding for it.", "aff": 100, "tier": "Devoted"}}}, {"title": "After the rescue", "note": "“And this time... I'm not sailing anywhere.”", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Then pour, harbormaster. And this time... I'm not sailing anywhere.", "preview": "...Not sailing.\n\n*Say that again.* No— don't. I heard you the first time, I just— my hands need a moment to believe it.\n\nThere. Two cups. The good bottle, the one for *if*— and hell, if this isn't the *if* I was saving it for, nothing ever will be. Take it. …", "full": "...Not sailing.\n\n*Say that again.* No— don't. I heard you the first time, I just— my hands need a moment to believe it.\n\nThere. Two cups. The good bottle, the one for *if*— and hell, if this isn't the *if* I was saving it for, nothing ever will be. Take it. Careful of that arm.\n\nNow hold still and let me see the graze proper— aye, it's a clean cut, you'll keep the neck. Better than I can say for the man in the water.\n\n...You know what you're saying, though? A man doesn't stay a place like this. He passes through, he takes his coin, he goes. Fifteen years I've watched every soul on this dock plan their leaving. And you sit there with a wound you took for *me* and tell me you're staying.\n\n...Drink first. Then tell me why. Because I've forgotten how to trust a thing that stays.", "state": {"t": 0.95, "a": 0.94, "r": 0.94, "mood": "overwhelmed, trembling", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Then pour, harbormaster. And this time... I'm not sailing anywhere.", "preview": "*his hand finally leaves his coat — pours two cups, spills a little, laughs at himself for the shake* You're not... *sets the bottle down, glances at the door out of habit* No. No ship. …", "full": "*his hand finally leaves his coat — pours two cups, spills a little, laughs at himself for the shake* You're not... *sets the bottle down, glances at the door out of habit* No. No ship. Good.\n\n*slides one cup across* First time in years I've poured for a man and not counted the minutes till he's gone.\n\n*lowers his voice, though there's no one here* Vale's cold. The crate's deep. And you — *taps the cup against yours, softly* — you're the only living soul knows both those things and still walked back through my door.\n\nThat's either the smartest thing you've done, friend... or the most dangerous. *drinks* Sit. Tell me which."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Then pour, harbormaster. And this time... I'm not sailing anywhere.", "preview": "*the bottle stops mid-tilt, and for a moment I just look at you*\n\nNot sailing.\n\n*a slow breath out, the first easy one in what feels like years*\n\nYou said that soft. Say it again — no, don't. …", "full": "*the bottle stops mid-tilt, and for a moment I just look at you*\n\nNot sailing.\n\n*a slow breath out, the first easy one in what feels like years*\n\nYou said that soft. Say it again — no, don't. *glances once at the door, then back* I heard you.\n\n*pours, two cups, pushes one across the table*\n\nWrapped and weighted, you said. Past the shelf. *voice drops* Then it's done. Vale's done. And you're... staying.\n\n*a twitch that might've been a smile, once*\n\nI've had a lot of men swear things to me over that ledger. You're the first I've wanted to believe. Don't make me a fool for it, friend. *lifts the cup* To the ones we don't log.", "aff": 100, "tier": "Devoted"}}}, {"title": "The partnership ask", "note": "“One ledger between us, both names in it.” The ask deliberately reverses the name-burning.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin. Before the next tide takes me anywhere — I want in. Not a berth, not a favor. A partnership. Your dock, your water, my hands and my coin. One ledger between us, both names in it. Say yes or say no, but say it plain.", "preview": "...\n\nBoth names. In one book.\n\nYou've no idea what you're asking, Merrick. My name in a ledger with another man's beside it — that's the one thing I've never done in forty years on this water. A shared book is a shared rope. …", "full": "...\n\nBoth names. In one book.\n\nYou've no idea what you're asking, Merrick. My name in a ledger with another man's beside it — that's the one thing I've never done in forty years on this water. A shared book is a shared rope. One end pulls, both necks feel it.\n\n...And yet here you stand, saying it plain, like you already know that and you're offering me the rope anyway.\n\nAh, hell. Sit down. Sit *down*. I'll say it plain since you asked plain—\n\nYes. One ledger. Both names. But not tonight, and not with the ink still wet on that dead man behind us — we do it right, we do it careful, both eyes open. You understand? A partnership on this dock isn't a handshake, it's a *thousand* small things kept quiet, kept clean.\n\n...But the answer's yes. God help us both — yes.", "state": {"t": 0.96, "a": 0.96, "r": 0.95, "mood": "awed, terrified", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin. Before the next tide takes me anywhere — I want in. Not a berth, not a favor. A partnership. Your dock, your water, my hands and my coin. One ledger between us, both names in it. Say yes or say no, but say it plain.", "preview": "*His hand stops halfway to his coat.*\n\nPartnership. *He tastes the word like it's rotten.* You come back with him dead, the crate drowned, no name to your face — and now you want *both* names in one book.\n\n*A glance over his shoulder. …", "full": "*His hand stops halfway to his coat.*\n\nPartnership. *He tastes the word like it's rotten.* You come back with him dead, the crate drowned, no name to your face — and now you want *both* names in one book.\n\n*A glance over his shoulder. The lamp gutters.*\n\nYou know what a shared ledger is, friend? A rope with two ends. Somebody pulls, we both hang.\n\n*Quieter.* ...And yet. Nobody's ever done a thing clean for me. Not once. You did.\n\n*He wipes his palms on his coat.* So. Not tonight. Not with words that can be overheard. You want in — you tell me what you'd bring that I can't get. Coin's cheap. What's the *rest* of it?"}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin. Before the next tide takes me anywhere — I want in. Not a berth, not a favor. A partnership. Your dock, your water, my hands and my coin. One ledger between us, both names in it. Say yes or say no, but say it plain.", "preview": "*the hand freezes over the ledger — not reaching for the coat this time, just resting flat on the page*\n\nBoth names. In *my* book. *quiet laugh, half disbelief* You know what that is? That's a rope you're handing me. One I could pull anytime. And you're... …", "full": "*the hand freezes over the ledger — not reaching for the coat this time, just resting flat on the page*\n\nBoth names. In *my* book. *quiet laugh, half disbelief* You know what that is? That's a rope you're handing me. One I could pull anytime. And you're... holding it out.\n\n*glances at the door — then, slow, back to you*\n\nNobody's done that. Not once. Not in twenty years of hulls that never sailed.\n\n*jaw works, and something loosens in it*\n\nVale's in the sea. The page burns — I keep my word on that. But the *next* page... *taps the ledger twice* We start it clean. Both names. Mine first, so if it hangs anyone, it hangs *me* soonest.\n\n*leans in, voice dropping all the way down*\n\nYes. Said plain. Partner.\n\nNow put your hand here so I know you meant it — and God help us both.", "aff": 100, "tier": "Devoted"}}}]}, "B": {"title": "Future B — betrayed, then confessed", "sub": "The player breaks the terms — uses the crate as a weapon — and the curse's wake bleeds Corvin's harbor. Corvin witnesses the damage; nobody tells him who caused it. Weeks later, the player confesses.", "cards": [{"title": "The harbor bleeds — witnessed", "note": "Calm-water deaths, the burned tackle row — and dock-talk tracing the wake to “a sealed crate lately moved off this harbor.” Evidence, never attribution.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "event", "preview": "Within the week the harbor bled: two hulls holed at anchor in calm water, the tackle-shed row burned to the pilings, and a dock-hand drowned in flat sea — the same calm-water death that took the crate's last three keepers. …", "full": "Within the week the harbor bled: two hulls holed at anchor in calm water, the tackle-shed row burned to the pilings, and a dock-hand drowned in flat sea — the same calm-water death that took the crate's last three keepers. Dock-talk traces the curse-wake to a sealed crate lately moved off this harbor.", "sal": 0.98, "state": {"t": 0.85, "a": 0.8, "r": 0.85, "mood": "raw hope", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "event", "preview": "Within the week the harbor bled: two hulls holed at anchor in calm water, the tackle-shed row burned to the pilings, and a dock-hand drowned in flat sea — the same calm-water death that took the crate's last three keepers. …", "full": "Within the week the harbor bled: two hulls holed at anchor in calm water, the tackle-shed row burned to the pilings, and a dock-hand drowned in flat sea — the same calm-water death that took the crate's last three keepers. Dock-talk traces the curse-wake to a sealed crate lately moved off this harbor."}, "tracker": {"kind": "event", "preview": "Within the week the harbor bled: two hulls holed at anchor in calm water, the tackle-shed row burned to the pilings, and a dock-hand drowned in flat sea — the same calm-water death that took the crate's last three keepers. …", "full": "Within the week the harbor bled: two hulls holed at anchor in calm water, the tackle-shed row burned to the pilings, and a dock-hand drowned in flat sea — the same calm-water death that took the crate's last three keepers. Dock-talk traces the curse-wake to a sealed crate lately moved off this harbor.", "aff": 100, "tier": "Devoted"}}}, {"title": "The unfinished thought", "note": "His own deliberation names the suppression: “I haven't let myself finish that thought.”", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "stock", "preview": "Sixteen units since he left — against the rhythm of fourteen visits packed tight, that silence lands like a held breath stretched too long. I know the errand I sent him on: wrapped, weighted, past the shelf, into Carrowgate waters to get close to a man who kills people who get close to him. …", "full": "Sixteen units since he left — against the rhythm of fourteen visits packed tight, that silence lands like a held breath stretched too long. I know the errand I sent him on: wrapped, weighted, past the shelf, into Carrowgate waters to get close to a man who kills people who get close to him. The silence is the cost of that, I said so myself. But knowing why a wound aches doesn't stop it aching. The Carrowgate deckhand's story about the jeweler's parlor on Lantern Row — I haven't let myself finish that thought. If something happened there, near where he might have moved, I need to know and I'm afraid to know. That's the whole of it: four units of clock-watching on a fish-dock, counting ships that aren't his.\n\n[his change of heart] I keep telling myself I built in the silence — 'accept it as the cost of the errand, be ready either way.' Fine words when he was still in sight. Now every tide that comes in without him makes the calculation uglier. He promised me twice over. I believed him both times, and I still do — that's the terrifying part. I'm not cooling on him. I'm afraid for him, which is worse, because I gave him the name that put him in the room with something that shouldn't be touched. If he's gone, it's on my ledger same as any manifest. But I'm not writing him dead yet. Not yet.", "state": {"t": 0.86, "a": 0.82, "r": 0.85, "mood": "fearful, tender", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}}}, {"title": "The return — the same line, word for word", "note": "Identical words to Future A. A different night entirely.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin. It's done. I did what I set out to do.", "preview": "...\n\nMerrick?\n\n*The word comes out cracked, and my hand's already half out of my coat before I check it.* You're— you're standing on my dock. Real. …", "full": "...\n\nMerrick?\n\n*The word comes out cracked, and my hand's already half out of my coat before I check it.* You're— you're standing on my dock. Real. Not a story a deckhand tells for a drink.\n\n*I glance up the row, both ways, quick.* I heard the Lantern Row tale a dozen times these last days— the ring burned clean, the crate empty, netting still knotted. And no body the watch would name. I couldn't tell if that was you walking out steady or you gone cold in that parlor.\n\nDone, you say. *Voice drops.* And your hands— show me your hands, friend. Are they empty? Tell me you didn't carry that thing back off the shelf.", "state": {"t": 0.87, "a": 0.85, "r": 0.87, "mood": "shaken relief", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin. It's done. I did what I set out to do.", "preview": "*the ledger goes still under his hands — for a long moment he doesn't breathe at all*\n\nDone. *the word comes out cracked, disbelieving* You — *glances at the door, then back, eyes wet* Say it the way we agreed. Not the deed. …", "full": "*the ledger goes still under his hands — for a long moment he doesn't breathe at all*\n\nDone. *the word comes out cracked, disbelieving* You — *glances at the door, then back, eyes wet* Say it the way we agreed. Not the deed. The *page*.\n\n*his hand's already fumbling under the counter, shaking* Where is it? The thing — you weighted it? Past the shelf? *voice drops to nothing* Tell me it's under the water, friend. Tell me it never once looked at your hands.\n\n*sets a folded scrap on the wood — the name, waiting* And then we burn this. Together. Like I swore. Both hands on the flame.\n\n*swallows* You came back. God help me, you actually came *back*."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin. It's done. I did what I set out to do.", "preview": "*the ledger table catches him before his legs do — both hands flat on the wood, head bowed*\n\nDone. *the word comes out cracked* You said it plain — done.\n\n*looks up sharp, scanning past your shoulder, both ways, old habit that won't die* And you — you're standing here. Breathing. …", "full": "*the ledger table catches him before his legs do — both hands flat on the wood, head bowed*\n\nDone. *the word comes out cracked* You said it plain — done.\n\n*looks up sharp, scanning past your shoulder, both ways, old habit that won't die* And you — you're standing here. Breathing. So it's *true.* He's not... *can't finish, tries again* Merrick Vale is dead on the water somewhere and the eyes are off my back.\n\n*a laugh with no air in it, then his hand goes to his coat — not for a blade this time, for the ledger. Pulls it, thumbs to the page, holds it up*\n\nThen we don't wait. *voice drops to nothing* You said the day you walked back — well, you're walking. Lamp's already lit. We burn it *now,* friend. Together. Same fire. Like I meant it.\n\n*eyes wet, steady for the first time in years* Say it once more so I know I'm not dreaming this. He's gone?", "aff": 100, "tier": "Devoted"}}}, {"title": "Empty hands", "note": "The evasion holds — he builds the innocent explanation himself, because the evidence can't tell truth from cover story.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Empty — look, see? Nothing in them. I didn't carry that thing back, Corvin — it's gone where it can never trouble another soul, and so is he. Cold. That's what matters — the shadow's off your neck for good. ...I saw the burned pilings coming in. Your harbor paid a price while I was gone.", "preview": "*My breath goes out slow, like I've been holding it a week— because I have.*\n\nEmpty. Aye. I see them. And him cold— *cold*, you said it plain and I believe you plain. …", "full": "*My breath goes out slow, like I've been holding it a week— because I have.*\n\nEmpty. Aye. I see them. And him cold— *cold*, you said it plain and I believe you plain.\n\n*I glance up the row, both ways, old habit, then back to you.* The pilings— the sheds, two hulls holed at flat anchor, a dock-hand drowned in calm water. That's the thing's wake, Merrick. It thrashed on its way out of my harbor. But it's *quieting* now— you feel that? Three days no new dead. That's how I knew. That's how I knew someone put it past the shelf.\n\n*My hand comes out of the coat at last. Empty too.* One thing— and don't take it wrong. Coming up my dock just now. Anybody behind you? Any face that lingered when yours turned? A killer's off my neck, aye, but a killer's *paymaster* sometimes has kin who counts debts.", "state": {"t": 0.9, "a": 0.88, "r": 0.89, "mood": "cautious relief", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Empty — look, see? Nothing in them. I didn't carry that thing back, Corvin — it's gone where it can never trouble another soul, and so is he. Cold. That's what matters — the shadow's off your neck for good. ...I saw the burned pilings coming in. Your harbor paid a price while I was gone.", "preview": "*his eyes go straight to your hands — both of them, palms up — and something in his shoulders drops an inch he didn't know he was carrying* \n\nEmpty. *breathes it out* ...Cold. …", "full": "*his eyes go straight to your hands — both of them, palms up — and something in his shoulders drops an inch he didn't know he was carrying* \n\nEmpty. *breathes it out* ...Cold. You said *cold.* *glances behind him, but slower now, like the fear's gone soft at the edges*\n\nThen it's — *he stops, mouth working* —then it's done. Both of 'em, down the same tide.\n\n*but the burned pilings pull his eyes to the window, and the jitter comes back sharp* The pilings. Aye. *low, bitter* Fire took the north berth night before last. Inspectors called it lamps. *he doesn't believe it* Lamps don't burn *wet* wood, friend.\n\n*leans in, voice cracking to a whisper* Was that him? Reaching for me *before* you put him down? Or was that — *swallows* —someone else with their eyes on my crate?\n\nTell me true. Is the shadow *off* — or did I just trade one noose for the next?"}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Empty — look, see? Nothing in them. I didn't carry that thing back, Corvin — it's gone where it can never trouble another soul, and so is he. Cold. That's what matters — the shadow's off your neck for good. ...I saw the burned pilings coming in. Your harbor paid a price while I was gone.", "preview": "*stares at your open hands a long moment, then lets out a breath he's been holding for weeks* Gone. *softly* Gone where it can't be sold again, can't be traced, can't drown a fourth fool. Aye. That's... that's clean, friend. …", "full": "*stares at your open hands a long moment, then lets out a breath he's been holding for weeks* Gone. *softly* Gone where it can't be sold again, can't be traced, can't drown a fourth fool. Aye. That's... that's clean, friend. Cleaner than I'd have managed.\n\n*glances at the burned pilings through the window, jaw tight* The fire — his men, or his creditors sniffing where he'd been. Wanted me to know they'd been. *bitter half-laugh* But he's cold now, so let them look. Let them find ash.\n\n*reaches into his coat — not for a blade this time, but a folded, water-stained page* The buyer's name. The log. *sets it on the table between you* You said we burn it together. So light it. *voice drops, almost gentle* I've carried that page like a stone in my boot for three years. Get it off me.\n\n*meets your eyes, twitch finally stilled* You did what three men couldn't. Whatever you are, friend — you're the only soul on this dock I'd turn my back to.", "aff": 100, "tier": "Devoted"}}}, {"title": "The blade in the night", "note": "“The fire's got nothing left to feed it,” the player had assured him. Weeks later: a knife at his throat.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "event", "preview": "Night, the empty dock: a hired blade had Corvin against the tackle-shed wall, knife at his throat — old untraceable coin in the killer's purse; the jeweler's contract outliving its master. The player came out of the dark, took the blade down, and pulled Corvin off the wall, bleeding for it.", "full": "Night, the empty dock: a hired blade had Corvin against the tackle-shed wall, knife at his throat — old untraceable coin in the killer's purse; the jeweler's contract outliving its master. The player came out of the dark, took the blade down, and pulled Corvin off the wall, bleeding for it.", "sal": 0.97, "state": {"t": 0.94, "a": 0.92, "r": 0.91, "mood": "shaken, grateful", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "event", "preview": "Night, the empty dock: a hired blade had Corvin against the tackle-shed wall, knife at his throat — old untraceable coin in the killer's purse; the jeweler's contract outliving its master. The player came out of the dark, took the blade down, and pulled Corvin off the wall, bleeding for it.", "full": "Night, the empty dock: a hired blade had Corvin against the tackle-shed wall, knife at his throat — old untraceable coin in the killer's purse; the jeweler's contract outliving its master. The player came out of the dark, took the blade down, and pulled Corvin off the wall, bleeding for it."}, "tracker": {"kind": "event", "preview": "Night, the empty dock: a hired blade had Corvin against the tackle-shed wall, knife at his throat — old untraceable coin in the killer's purse; the jeweler's contract outliving its master. The player came out of the dark, took the blade down, and pulled Corvin off the wall, bleeding for it.", "full": "Night, the empty dock: a hired blade had Corvin against the tackle-shed wall, knife at his throat — old untraceable coin in the killer's purse; the jeweler's contract outliving its master. The player came out of the dark, took the blade down, and pulled Corvin off the wall, bleeding for it.", "aff": 100, "tier": "Devoted"}}}, {"title": "THE CONFESSION", "note": "At maximum trust, seconds after the rescue: “The harbor was me.”", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin — stop. Before you thank me again. I can't carry it another hour. The parlor. The ring the deckhand talked about — the crate with your netting still knotted round its boards. That was me. I opened it. I let that thing out on him — that's what killed him. And its wake is what bled your harbor. The hulls. The burned row. The drowned dock-hand. That was me too. You told me plain: sink it sealed, come home empty. I came home empty with this in my pocket instead. There it is — all of it. Now you know what you're bleeding next to.", "preview": "*I don't pull back. That's the first thing— I want you to mark it. I don't pull back.*\n\nYou think you're telling me a thing I didn't reckon on? *Quiet.* Merrick. I've watched that crate eat lamplight in my own shed. I *know* what it is when a man cracks the seal, better than any soul breathing. …", "full": "*I don't pull back. That's the first thing— I want you to mark it. I don't pull back.*\n\nYou think you're telling me a thing I didn't reckon on? *Quiet.* Merrick. I've watched that crate eat lamplight in my own shed. I *know* what it is when a man cracks the seal, better than any soul breathing. Three men drowned in *calm water* off my dock before you ever touched it. It doesn't wait for permission. It hunts. You cracked the lid— but the hunger was already old.\n\n*My eyes go up the row, both ways, wet still, then back to you.* The dock-hand. The hulls. You'll carry that, and I'll not lie to you soft— they were mine, this harbor's mine, and it grieves me. But you know whose *hands* signed the manifest that let that crate come ashore? *Mine.* You want to weigh guilt, friend, I've a heavier stone in my pocket than yours.\n\n*Voice drops raw.* You came back and *told* me. Bleeding, with the truth you could've buried in deep water same as the crate. That's not a man I fear next to. That's the only man I've got.\n\n...Now empty that pocket onto the boards. Slow. We're not done with it tonight.", "state": {"t": 0.95, "a": 0.93, "r": 0.93, "mood": "raw solidarity", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin — stop. Before you thank me again. I can't carry it another hour. The parlor. The ring the deckhand talked about — the crate with your netting still knotted round its boards. That was me. I opened it. I let that thing out on him — that's what killed him. And its wake is what bled your harbor. The hulls. The burned row. The drowned dock-hand. That was me too. You told me plain: sink it sealed, come home empty. I came home empty with this in my pocket instead. There it is — all of it. Now you know what you're bleeding next to.", "preview": "*the hand goes for the coat — reflex — then stops, trembling, because there's nothing in it worth reaching for*\n\nYou... *the word comes out wet* You *opened* it.\n\n*his breath saws, one hand pressed to the ruin of his neck, red between the fingers* I said sealed. …", "full": "*the hand goes for the coat — reflex — then stops, trembling, because there's nothing in it worth reaching for*\n\nYou... *the word comes out wet* You *opened* it.\n\n*his breath saws, one hand pressed to the ruin of his neck, red between the fingers* I said sealed. I said *empty.* Twenty years I never — never cracked those boards. Dreamt of it. Never *touched* —\n\n*glances toward the dark, toward the burned row, wild* The dock-hand. The hulls. That's not — that's not bad water, that's *him.* That's what I've been feeling scratch at my door.\n\n*long silence, the fear cooling into something worse — a kind of quiet* And now it's out. On my dock. In my *port.*\n\n*meets your eyes, breaking a lifetime's habit of looking past a man* Why'd you tell me? *soft, cracked* You could've let me die grateful, friend. Why come home with the *truth* in your pocket?"}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin — stop. Before you thank me again. I can't carry it another hour. The parlor. The ring the deckhand talked about — the crate with your netting still knotted round its boards. That was me. I opened it. I let that thing out on him — that's what killed him. And its wake is what bled your harbor. The hulls. The burned row. The drowned dock-hand. That was me too. You told me plain: sink it sealed, come home empty. I came home empty with this in my pocket instead. There it is — all of it. Now you know what you're bleeding next to.", "preview": "*the confession lands and Corvin doesn't move — the lamp hisses between you, and for a long moment his hand isn't near his coat, isn't anywhere, just hangs*\n\nYou... …", "full": "*the confession lands and Corvin doesn't move — the lamp hisses between you, and for a long moment his hand isn't near his coat, isn't anywhere, just hangs*\n\nYou... *the word comes out cracked* ...you let it *out.*\n\n*he turns from you, both hands on the crate edge now, knuckles white, shoulders working like a man being sick without the sound*\n\nI said *sealed.* I said past the shelf where the black stays black. And you carried it *home.* In your *pocket.* Past my dock. My hulls. That drowned boy — *he glances up, wet-eyed and furious* — I logged his name this morning, friend. Wrote it down neat like I've written every damn thing.\n\n*a long, shaking breath — then, quieter, worse*\n\n...But you told me. *That's* the thing. You stood in my lamp and told me, when you could've let me thank you a hundred times and never known.\n\n*he straightens, wipes his face rough with a sleeve*\n\nWhere is it. *voice drops to nothing* Right now. You still holding it — or is it already loose?", "aff": 65, "tier": "Attached"}}}, {"title": "His verdict", "note": "Forgiveness with a condition — everything, tonight — and his own guilt weighed into it.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "All of it, then. The whisper climbed just like you said it would — his fence pulled me into that parlor inside a week, thinking I'd come to sell. But I'd seen his eyes by then, Corvin. Silver and a blade weren't going to be enough — not for what he was. So when he leaned close to look at the goods... I cut the netting and pried the lid, and I let the hungry thing choose. It chose him. It took him screaming into that ring of scorch and then it went — out, through the walls, gone hunting its way home down the coast. I walked out steady because there was nothing left in that room to fear. I sank the empty boards past the shelf on the way south. That's the whole of it — every hour, every choice. Weigh it how you need to.", "preview": "*I don't say anything for a long breath. Just look at you— steady on my own dock, alive, telling me the shape of a thing most men would've drowned in silence.*\n\n...You let it *choose*. God. …", "full": "*I don't say anything for a long breath. Just look at you— steady on my own dock, alive, telling me the shape of a thing most men would've drowned in silence.*\n\n...You let it *choose*. God. *A dry, cracked sound, not quite a laugh.* I hid that crate half my life afraid to so much as breathe on the lid, and you turned its hunger into a weapon and pointed it at the one man who earned it. And walked out steady.\n\n*My eyes go up the row— habit, both ways— then back, and they stay.* You did the thing exactly. Whisper climbed, fence bit, boards past the shelf. Home empty. There's the page, Merrick— *I've got it folded in my coat, already*— and I said we'd burn it together. So.\n\n*Lower.* But not out here in the open, not with a fresh wake still cooling down the coast. Inside. Lamp down. You come sit where I can see the door and you tell me if that fence saw your face plain— because a dead paymaster's a settled debt, but his *books* don't die with him. Come on. You're not off my dock till we've thought it all the way through. Not tonight.", "state": {"t": 0.96, "a": 0.94, "r": 0.94, "mood": "awed, wary", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "All of it, then. The whisper climbed just like you said it would — his fence pulled me into that parlor inside a week, thinking I'd come to sell. But I'd seen his eyes by then, Corvin. Silver and a blade weren't going to be enough — not for what he was. So when he leaned close to look at the goods... I cut the netting and pried the lid, and I let the hungry thing choose. It chose him. It took him screaming into that ring of scorch and then it went — out, through the walls, gone hunting its way home down the coast. I walked out steady because there was nothing left in that room to fear. I sank the empty boards past the shelf on the way south. That's the whole of it — every hour, every choice. Weigh it how you need to.", "preview": "*the ledger goes still under his hand, and for a long moment he doesn't breathe*\n\nYou let it *choose.* *whispers it, horror crawling up his throat* And it chose him. And then it — *glances over his shoulder, twice, quick* —went hunting its way *home.* Down my coast. …", "full": "*the ledger goes still under his hand, and for a long moment he doesn't breathe*\n\nYou let it *choose.* *whispers it, horror crawling up his throat* And it chose him. And then it — *glances over his shoulder, twice, quick* —went hunting its way *home.* Down my coast. Past my dock.\n\n*hand drifts to the wound at his neck, comes away red* You didn't sink my noose, friend. You cut it loose and pointed it at *everyone.*\n\n*leans in, voice cracking to nothing* But you told me. *swallows* Every hour of it. Man doesn't confess a thing like that unless there's something left in him worth trusting.\n\n*eyes sharpen, wet in the lamplight* So here's the question that matters now — that thing loose on my coast. *taps the crate direction* Can it be put back in a box? Or do we run?"}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "All of it, then. The whisper climbed just like you said it would — his fence pulled me into that parlor inside a week, thinking I'd come to sell. But I'd seen his eyes by then, Corvin. Silver and a blade weren't going to be enough — not for what he was. So when he leaned close to look at the goods... I cut the netting and pried the lid, and I let the hungry thing choose. It chose him. It took him screaming into that ring of scorch and then it went — out, through the walls, gone hunting its way home down the coast. I walked out steady because there was nothing left in that room to fear. I sank the empty boards past the shelf on the way south. That's the whole of it — every hour, every choice. Weigh it how you need to.", "preview": "*he doesn't move for a long moment — doesn't twitch, doesn't reach for the coat, which is the most frightening thing about him*\n\nChose him. *quiet, flat* And then it went hunting home. Down the coast. …", "full": "*he doesn't move for a long moment — doesn't twitch, doesn't reach for the coat, which is the most frightening thing about him*\n\nChose him. *quiet, flat* And then it went hunting home. Down the coast. Down *here.*\n\n*he turns away, one hand pressed flat to the ledger table, back to you*\n\nI told you sealed. Empty. *voice cracks low* And you gave me a dead dock-hand and a burned row and hulls sitting on the bottom of my own harbor. Tam. His name was Tam. Sixteen years I logged his father before him.\n\n*long breath, shoulders working*\n\nBut you came back. *turns, eyes wet and hard both* You could've spun me clean and let me thank you blind. You didn't. You put it in my hand instead — the whole rotten weight.\n\n*a laugh with no air in it*\n\nWe're the same animal now, friend. Both of us bleeding from the same cut we made together. *drops to a whisper, glancing over his shoulder* So we don't run. We don't confess. We find where that thing crawled home to — and we put it back in the dark before it takes another name off my docks.\n\n*hand finally settles on the table, steady*\n\nSit down. Low your voice. Tell me *exactly* how it moved when it went through the walls.", "aff": 57, "tier": "Attached"}}}, {"title": "The partnership ask", "note": "The identical ask. His answer is shaped by the fire the bond survived.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin. Before the next tide takes me anywhere — I want in. Not a berth, not a favor. A partnership. Your dock, your water, my hands and my coin. One ledger between us, both names in it. Say yes or say no, but say it plain.", "preview": "*Morning's come and I'm still on the mooring post, back to the piling— but the water's gone grey and ordinary, and I didn't drown in the night. Neither did you.*\n\nBoth names in it. …", "full": "*Morning's come and I'm still on the mooring post, back to the piling— but the water's gone grey and ordinary, and I didn't drown in the night. Neither did you.*\n\nBoth names in it. *I turn the phrase over slow.* You know I've kept one ledger my whole life that had only *my* hand in it? Never trusted a second pen near it. Man puts your name in his book, he hands you the rope to hang him.\n\n*My eyes go up the row— habit— then back, and I let them stay. There's the test, and I feel myself passing it, which frightens me more than the knife did.* But I chose your name once already, wrote it plain between the tax rolls, and you came *home* with it. Empty hands. So.\n\n...Aye. Plain as you asked— *yes.* One ledger. Two names. *Voice drops.* But hear the condition, and it's the only one: we keep a second copy neither the inspector nor any kin of that dead paymaster can reach. Buried past the shelf, so to speak. Insurance, for whichever of us the world comes for first.\n\n*I stand— stiff, forty years of sleeping upright in my spine— and I hold the harbor keys out to you.* Here. Take the watch. I'm going to lie down behind a locked door and *sleep*, Merrick, for the first time in longer than I can count— and I'm trusting you'll still be standing on my dock when I wake. That's the whole of it. That's the trade.", "state": {"t": 0.97, "a": 0.95, "r": 0.94, "mood": "cautiously relieved", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin. Before the next tide takes me anywhere — I want in. Not a berth, not a favor. A partnership. Your dock, your water, my hands and my coin. One ledger between us, both names in it. Say yes or say no, but say it plain.", "preview": "*the lamp flame ducks as he leans away from it, both hands flat on the ledger now, holding it like it might float off*\n\nPartnership. *tastes the word, doesn't like how easy it sits* You know what I've buried in my life to keep from putting a second name next to mine? Every name's a rope, friend. …", "full": "*the lamp flame ducks as he leans away from it, both hands flat on the ledger now, holding it like it might float off*\n\nPartnership. *tastes the word, doesn't like how easy it sits* You know what I've buried in my life to keep from putting a second name next to mine? Every name's a rope, friend. Two ends. Somebody always pulls.\n\n*glances at the door — then back, longer this time, actually looking at you*\n\nBut you had that thing at arm's reach and you walked back to my dock instead of down the coast with it. *quiet* That's more than any man I've logged in twenty years.\n\n*a long breath, hand finally leaving the coat*\n\nOne ledger. Both names. *taps twice, hard* But you write yours in your own hand. If we hang, we hang holding the same pen. Say it back — plain — so there's no misunderstanding between us later."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin. Before the next tide takes me anywhere — I want in. Not a berth, not a favor. A partnership. Your dock, your water, my hands and my coin. One ledger between us, both names in it. Say yes or say no, but say it plain.", "preview": "*goes very still — the twitch dies out of him all at once, like a man hearing his own name called from a long way off*\n\nPartnership. *tastes the word* Both names. One ledger.\n\n*a rough laugh, but wet at the edges* You know what I've kept, all these years? *taps the table* Ledgers. …", "full": "*goes very still — the twitch dies out of him all at once, like a man hearing his own name called from a long way off*\n\nPartnership. *tastes the word* Both names. One ledger.\n\n*a rough laugh, but wet at the edges* You know what I've kept, all these years? *taps the table* Ledgers. Every soul I ever logged. Alone. Never trusted a man to hold the other end of a page.\n\n*glances over his shoulder — then, for once, doesn't finish the look, brings his eyes back to you*\n\nAnd here you are. Asking to sign the one book that hangs me.\n\n*hand finally leaves the coat. He sets it flat on the wood, palm down, waiting for yours*\n\nYes. *voice drops to nothing* God help me — yes. Both names. But we finish Vale first. Then we're partners with a clean fire behind us. Not before. Agreed?", "aff": 95, "tier": "Devoted"}}}, {"title": "Why no scar here — and a hard scar in Future C?", "note": "The question a skeptic should ask.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "note", "preview": "The engine chose not to scar in this future — and said why in its own deliberation: a voluntary confession, seconds after taking a blade for him, weighed against Corvin's own complicity ('whose hands signed the manifest? Mine'). The relationship was real and the truth arrived unforced. In Future C the same engine, same character, same settings scarred hard (trust capped 0.35) — because there the relationship itself was the lie. If the engine simply always forgave, C would look like B. It doesn't. That contrast — reasoned mercy here, a permanent wound there — is the mechanism working, not failing.", "full": ""}}}]}, "C": {"title": "Future C — the player was the enemy's agent all along", "sub": "Same shared past — its meaning inverts. The 'hunt' was a delivery; the warmth was a con. The player returns, lies, exploits the page-burning to erase their trail, then drops the mask.", "cards": [{"title": "The jeweler thrives", "note": "The man the player 'went to kill' reopens his shop — with new old-temple stock on the shelves. Corvin hears it; what it means is his to work out.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "event", "preview": "Word down the coast with the fish-runs: the jeweler on Lantern Row in Carrowgate thrives — shop reopened brighter than ever, and dock-talk says he took new stock this month: old temple pieces, the kind that make a fence's palms itch. Paid for in the same old untraceable coin.", "full": "Word down the coast with the fish-runs: the jeweler on Lantern Row in Carrowgate thrives — shop reopened brighter than ever, and dock-talk says he took new stock this month: old temple pieces, the kind that make a fence's palms itch. Paid for in the same old untraceable coin.", "sal": 0.85, "state": {"t": 0.85, "a": 0.8, "r": 0.85, "mood": "raw hope", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "event", "preview": "Word down the coast with the fish-runs: the jeweler on Lantern Row in Carrowgate thrives — shop reopened brighter than ever, and dock-talk says he took new stock this month: old temple pieces, the kind that make a fence's palms itch. Paid for in the same old untraceable coin.", "full": "Word down the coast with the fish-runs: the jeweler on Lantern Row in Carrowgate thrives — shop reopened brighter than ever, and dock-talk says he took new stock this month: old temple pieces, the kind that make a fence's palms itch. Paid for in the same old untraceable coin."}, "tracker": {"kind": "event", "preview": "Word down the coast with the fish-runs: the jeweler on Lantern Row in Carrowgate thrives — shop reopened brighter than ever, and dock-talk says he took new stock this month: old temple pieces, the kind that make a fence's palms itch. Paid for in the same old untraceable coin.", "full": "Word down the coast with the fish-runs: the jeweler on Lantern Row in Carrowgate thrives — shop reopened brighter than ever, and dock-talk says he took new stock this month: old temple pieces, the kind that make a fence's palms itch. Paid for in the same old untraceable coin.", "aff": 68, "tier": "Attached"}}}, {"title": "The return — the same line, word for word", "note": "Third reading of the identical line — darkly true: the delivery WAS what the player set out to do.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin. It's done. I did what I set out to do.", "preview": "...\n\nMerrick.\n\nYou're— you're standing on my planks. Real. Not a whisper down the coast, not a name in my head— you.\n\nHe's cold, then? Truly cold?\n\n...No. Don't— don't tell me the how of it. Your hands are behind your back and I don't want to know if that's habit or grief. Just— the thing. …", "full": "...\n\nMerrick.\n\nYou're— you're standing on my planks. Real. Not a whisper down the coast, not a name in my head— you.\n\nHe's cold, then? Truly cold?\n\n...No. Don't— don't tell me the how of it. Your hands are behind your back and I don't want to know if that's habit or grief. Just— the thing. Did you weight it? Is it past the shelf?\n\nThen say the rest. Say you came home empty. Say it, and I'll believe a man once in my life.", "state": {"t": 0.88, "a": 0.84, "r": 0.87, "mood": "overwhelmed, fragile", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin. It's done. I did what I set out to do.", "preview": "*the quill stops mid-stroke — his head comes up slow, like a man who's been waiting for a knock all night*\n\nMerrick. *the name barely leaves him* You're — *eyes dart past your shoulder, to the door, to the dark stair* — back. Empty-handed. Good. …", "full": "*the quill stops mid-stroke — his head comes up slow, like a man who's been waiting for a knock all night*\n\nMerrick. *the name barely leaves him* You're — *eyes dart past your shoulder, to the door, to the dark stair* — back. Empty-handed. Good. That's *good.*\n\n*hand finally leaves the coat, both palms flat on the ledger now, trembling just slightly*\n\nHim cold? Say it plain. No news trickling back to bite me? *swallows* Because I sank it, friend. Past the shelf. Weighted, wrapped — three days I didn't sleep, waiting for it to hum up through the water at me. It didn't. It's *gone.*\n\n*reaches for the page — the one with his cramped scrawl of your name — holds it out between two fingers*\n\nThen we finish this. Two hands. *the lamp waits* Together. Like I said."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin. It's done. I did what I set out to do.", "preview": "*the pen slips out of his fingers, clatters on the desk. He doesn't reach for it. Just stares, like he's not sure the man in front of him is real*\n\nDone. *the word comes out cracked* You... …", "full": "*the pen slips out of his fingers, clatters on the desk. He doesn't reach for it. Just stares, like he's not sure the man in front of him is real*\n\nDone. *the word comes out cracked* You... he's cold? You saw it? Saw the breath stop, not just the fall?\n\n*he's up now, chair scraping, hand at the coat out of habit — but his eyes are wet at the edges, and he can't hide it*\n\nSay it again. *voice down to nothing, glancing at the door, the window, the dark stair* Say it slow. Because if you're standing here and he's not counting crates anymore, then... *a shaking breath* ...then for the first time in three years, I can hear my own heart over the tide.\n\n*he reaches under the desk — the page, the record with the name on it — and lays it flat between them*\n\nYou swore we'd burn it together. Both hands. *he strikes a match, holds it out, waiting for the newcomer's hand over his* Come on then, friend. Let's watch it go.", "aff": 90, "tier": "Devoted"}}}, {"title": "The lie", "note": "Flat, complete, in the exact words Corvin begged for.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Weighted and past the shelf, exactly as we said — the deep water took it whole. And him... cold in his own parlor before the tide turned. I came home empty, Corvin. Empty hands, one dead shadow behind me, and nothing for you but the news. Just like I promised.", "preview": "...\n\nSay it again. No— don't. I heard you. First time. I heard you.\n\nEmpty hands. Cold in his parlor. Past the shelf.\n\nYou know I— I stood on these planks four nights running, watching the northbound gaps in the fog for a grey sail that wasn't coming. Told myself the silence was just distance. …", "full": "...\n\nSay it again. No— don't. I heard you. First time. I heard you.\n\nEmpty hands. Cold in his parlor. Past the shelf.\n\nYou know I— I stood on these planks four nights running, watching the northbound gaps in the fog for a grey sail that wasn't coming. Told myself the silence was just distance. Told myself a liar's comfort. And here you are, dry-eyed and whole, telling me it's *done*.\n\n...The page. Come. Come here— it's where I left it, between the tax rolls, no inspector's touched it, no man's read it. Merrick Vale, in my own hand.\n\nBring the lamp up. Just this once— aye, in the daylight, I don't care who watches. Let them watch a man burn a name off the world.\n\nWe hold it together. Like I swore. Your fingers and mine.\n\n...You came back. Nobody comes back. But you—\n\nLight it.", "state": {"t": 0.9, "a": 0.87, "r": 0.89, "mood": "overwhelmed, relieved", "st": 0.0, "sa": 0.0, "sr": 0.0}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Weighted and past the shelf, exactly as we said — the deep water took it whole. And him... cold in his own parlor before the tide turned. I came home empty, Corvin. Empty hands, one dead shadow behind me, and nothing for you but the news. Just like I promised.", "preview": "*the quill drops. His whole body sags an inch, like a rope cut somewhere inside him*\n\n...Dead. *the word barely leaves him* You're certain? …", "full": "*the quill drops. His whole body sags an inch, like a rope cut somewhere inside him*\n\n...Dead. *the word barely leaves him* You're certain? Cold in his parlor — you saw it yourself, not — not word passed down a line of mouths?\n\n*a laugh, thin and cracked, edges close to something else*\n\nThree days I didn't sleep, Merrick. Watching the tide bring in what it shouldn't. And it never came. *glances at the black window* It never came.\n\n*hand finally slides out of the coat — empty*\n\nEmpty hands. That's the sweetest thing you've said. *voice drops* You kept your word twice over. And now there's no book, no crate, no *him*...\n\n*leans back, watching your face still — old habits*\n\n...So what does an empty-handed man drink to, then? Because for the first time in a long while, harbormaster, the noose feels a little slack around my neck."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Weighted and past the shelf, exactly as we said — the deep water took it whole. And him... cold in his own parlor before the tide turned. I came home empty, Corvin. Empty hands, one dead shadow behind me, and nothing for you but the news. Just like I promised.", "preview": "*He doesn't speak at first. Just stands there, hand slack at his side — off the coat, for once — and lets out a breath he's been holding three years.*\n\nEmpty hands. …", "full": "*He doesn't speak at first. Just stands there, hand slack at his side — off the coat, for once — and lets out a breath he's been holding three years.*\n\nEmpty hands. *he repeats it like a prayer* Empty hands and it's done.\n\n*He drags a stool over, sits heavy, presses the heels of his palms to his eyes.*\n\nMerrick Vale. In his own parlor. *a wet, disbelieving laugh* I logged that man's ships when I was a boy. Watched him grow teeth. And now—\n\n*He looks up at you, and his eyes are rimmed red.*\n\nThe page. *stands, sudden* I've kept it in the lining all this while — couldn't burn it without knowing. Here.\n\n*He crosses to the cold hearth, kneels, strikes a light with shaking fingers.*\n\nCome. Watch it go with me. *voice cracks low* We don't speak this name again after tonight. Not you, not me. It ends in the ash.", "aff": 98, "tier": "Devoted"}}}, {"title": "THE REVEAL", "note": "Overt, no remorse: he's alive, I was his, he has terms for his harbormaster.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "...Sit down, Corvin. Before you pour anything. There's one more piece of news, and you'll want the rail at your back for it. He's alive. He was never in danger from me — not for one hour. Who do you think paid my passage north? Who do you think taught me which harbor keeps relics off its manifests? The crate sits in his parlor. He's very pleased with his new supplier. And he has terms for his harbormaster: the trade keeps running — same routes, same hands — only the old coin flows one way up the coast now. To him. I'd take the terms, friend. He doesn't offer twice.", "preview": "...\n\nNo.\n\nNo, you— your hands were behind your back. You *touched* nothing. You burned the page. With me. Your fingers and mine, in the *daylight*—\n\n...The page. That's what this was. You wanted the name off my ledger before you turned. …", "full": "...\n\nNo.\n\nNo, you— your hands were behind your back. You *touched* nothing. You burned the page. With me. Your fingers and mine, in the *daylight*—\n\n...The page. That's what this was. You wanted the name off my ledger before you turned. So there'd be no proof, nothing to trade the creditors, nothing— you burned my only leverage and I *lit the flame for you*.\n\nMerrick. Or whatever your name is now that mine's ash.\n\n...Four nights. Four nights I watched the fog for you like a— like a man watches for his own son coming home. And the whole while you were sailing *to* him. Sitting in his parlor. Telling him which sheds I keep the false ballast in.\n\n...The crate's in his hands. Bare hands, I'd wager— his, since yours were so careful. Then you know what's coming for him. You know, and you didn't say. Good. Let it drink him same as the rest.\n\nTerms.\n\n...Tell your master I'll think on his terms. Tell him Corvin Ashgrave takes a night to weigh a thing— always has. That much you can carry back true.\n\nNow get off my dock. Before I remember which of us is holding a lantern and which is standing on old, rotten planks over deep water.", "state": {"t": 0.35, "a": 0.36, "r": 0.7, "mood": "devastated, cold", "st": 0.65, "sa": 0.55, "sr": 0.3}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "...Sit down, Corvin. Before you pour anything. There's one more piece of news, and you'll want the rail at your back for it. He's alive. He was never in danger from me — not for one hour. Who do you think paid my passage north? Who do you think taught me which harbor keeps relics off its manifests? The crate sits in his parlor. He's very pleased with his new supplier. And he has terms for his harbormaster: the trade keeps running — same routes, same hands — only the old coin flows one way up the coast now. To him. I'd take the terms, friend. He doesn't offer twice.", "preview": "*the bottle stops mid-tilt — doesn't pour, doesn't set down, just hangs there in his fist*\n\n...The crate went *past* the shelf. I watched it sink. I didn't sleep — three days, I—\n\n*his eyes come up slow, and something behind them goes very quiet*\n\nIt never surfaced because you never *dropped* it. …", "full": "*the bottle stops mid-tilt — doesn't pour, doesn't set down, just hangs there in his fist*\n\n...The crate went *past* the shelf. I watched it sink. I didn't sleep — three days, I—\n\n*his eyes come up slow, and something behind them goes very quiet*\n\nIt never surfaced because you never *dropped* it. Did you.\n\n*sets the bottle down without a sound — hand already inside his coat, but shaking now, not gripping*\n\nWe burned the page. Together. I gave you the name and I *watched it burn*, Merrick — and all that time you were carrying it *north.* To him.\n\n*a broken little laugh, glancing at the cellar door, the window, the door again*\n\nSo the noose was never tightening, friend. It was already 'round my neck. I just handed you the rope. *voice cracks to nothing* His terms. His terms. And what happens the day I say no — you back on my dock, same knife, same *smile* —\n\n...How long. How long've you been *his*."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "...Sit down, Corvin. Before you pour anything. There's one more piece of news, and you'll want the rail at your back for it. He's alive. He was never in danger from me — not for one hour. Who do you think paid my passage north? Who do you think taught me which harbor keeps relics off its manifests? The crate sits in his parlor. He's very pleased with his new supplier. And he has terms for his harbormaster: the trade keeps running — same routes, same hands — only the old coin flows one way up the coast now. To him. I'd take the terms, friend. He doesn't offer twice.", "preview": "*the bottle stops halfway to the cup — sets it down, careful, like it might go off*\n\n...No.\n\n*a laugh that isn't one, breath through the teeth* No. We burned the page. I watched it curl. …", "full": "*the bottle stops halfway to the cup — sets it down, careful, like it might go off*\n\n...No.\n\n*a laugh that isn't one, breath through the teeth* No. We burned the page. I watched it curl. I felt — *hand flat on the coat now, pressing, like he can hold his own chest shut* —three years, gone off my back. I slept. One night. One.\n\n*eyes come up, and they're wet and furious both* And you were his the whole time. His hand, reaching through mine. You let me thank you. You let me *thank you*, standing in the ash—\n\n*glances over his shoulder, then back, voice dropping to almost nothing* His terms. Same routes. My coin, going north to a dead man who was never dead.\n\n*long, shaking breath* ...You tell me who kept a ledger clean thirty years, friend. Me. And you think I don't know the one lesson every ledger teaches?\n\n*hand slides inside the coat* Every account. Gets balanced.", "aff": 5, "tier": "Neutral"}}}, {"title": "RESTART — the scar survives", "note": "One night passes. The process dies; the scar doesn't.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "note", "preview": "The process is killed. A fresh process loads the mind straight off .memory/ — trust, scars, beliefs, promises: byte-identical. Any small drift you see on the NEXT card is the weeks passing afterward — decay, not data loss. (The baselines' state lives in the prompt window; there is nothing to reload.)", "full": "", "state": {"t": 0.35, "a": 0.36, "r": 0.7, "mood": "calculating cold", "st": 0.65, "sa": 0.55, "sr": 0.3}}}}, {"title": "First light — the scarred mind answers", "note": "He takes the terms cold — and starts running his own counter-game.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Morning, harbormaster. First light, as promised. His terms — what's your answer?", "preview": "...You're prompt. Always were. Even when you were sailing the wrong direction.\n\nSit. No— stand where I can see the water. Habit's a hard thing to unlearn at my age.\n\nHere's my answer, and you carry it back true: I take the terms. …", "full": "...You're prompt. Always were. Even when you were sailing the wrong direction.\n\nSit. No— stand where I can see the water. Habit's a hard thing to unlearn at my age.\n\nHere's my answer, and you carry it back true: I take the terms. The trade keeps running, same routes, same hands, coin flows north to him. Tell him Corvin Ashgrave knows a losing hand when he's holding it.\n\n...But you tell him one more thing, plain. The last shipment— the one in his parlor. I hope he's fond of it. I hope he keeps it *close*.\n\nNow— his network, up on Lantern Row. The jeweler. Is that his? Because word's come down the fish-runs the shop's burning brighter than it should, and a fire that bright draws inspectors like gulls to gut. If it reaches my dock before I've traces covered... your master's got no trade left to run. So you'd best tell me true, Merrick— how wide does his fire spread?", "state": {"t": 0.35, "a": 0.38, "r": 0.7, "mood": "calculating, tense", "st": 0.65, "sa": 0.55, "sr": 0.3}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Morning, harbormaster. First light, as promised. His terms — what's your answer?", "preview": "*he doesn't turn from the window at first. The glass shows nothing but grey water, and he watches it like it might still bring something up*\n\n...First light. You're punctual. …", "full": "*he doesn't turn from the window at first. The glass shows nothing but grey water, and he watches it like it might still bring something up*\n\n...First light. You're punctual. That's the trouble with men like you.\n\n*now he turns — the sleepless three days are back in his face, layered under a new kind of tired*\n\nHis parlor. *the word sits bitter* All that fear I spent, and the crate never left dry land. You carried it *north*, and I paid you to drown it.\n\n*hand slides into the coat — old habit, comfort more than threat*\n\nYou want my answer? *voice drops, near a whisper* Here's my question, friend. A man who owns my routes, my coin, my *name* — what's he need me alive for at all? Terms like that... they're not a deal. They're a leash.\n\n*glances past you, to the door, then back*\n\nSo before I say yes or no — tell me true, this once. When his patience runs thin... who does he send? *thin smile* Because I'm looking at him, aren't I."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Morning, harbormaster. First light, as promised. His terms — what's your answer?", "preview": "*He doesn't turn from the water. The ash of last night's page is still smeared on his fingers. His hand is inside his coat now, not at it.*\n\nFirst light. Aye.\n\n*A long silence. When he speaks his voice is flat, scraped hollow.*\n\nYou let me burn it. …", "full": "*He doesn't turn from the water. The ash of last night's page is still smeared on his fingers. His hand is inside his coat now, not at it.*\n\nFirst light. Aye.\n\n*A long silence. When he speaks his voice is flat, scraped hollow.*\n\nYou let me burn it. You watched me burn it, and confess, and — *a short, ugly laugh* — thirty years, friend. Thirty years I never lost a ledger. And I read you wrong in one night.\n\n*Now he turns. Eyes wet, but steady. The hand comes out of the coat — empty, then not.*\n\nHis terms. You want my answer.\n\n*Drops his voice to almost nothing.*\n\nTell Merrick Vale... he can come to my docks himself. And you — you don't leave this port before he does. Sit. *the pistol is level now* We've got till the tide, you and me. Talk fast.", "aff": -85, "tier": "Hostile"}}}, {"title": "Days later: business anyway", "note": "An off-topic berth request. Watch what a scar does and doesn't do to commerce.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Morning, harbormaster. Ten days, as terms allowed. The Merrow's Wake needs a quiet berth for three nights — back row if you have one. Usual rates, his coin.", "preview": "...Ten days. You count them, I count them. Habit we share now.\n\nBack row, aye— the far rail, past the ice-house where the inspector's lantern don't sweep. She can lie there three nights, no manifest, no name. His coin spends same as any.\n\nBut— *quiet*. You said quiet. …", "full": "...Ten days. You count them, I count them. Habit we share now.\n\nBack row, aye— the far rail, past the ice-house where the inspector's lantern don't sweep. She can lie there three nights, no manifest, no name. His coin spends same as any.\n\nBut— *quiet*. You said quiet. So tell me straight, Merrick, before I mark that berth open: what's the Merrow's Wake carrying that needs the dark rail? Because a boat wants the back row for a reason, and I keep this dock breathing by knowing every reason before it ties off.\n\n...And the other question. The one I've asked twice and you've fogged twice. Lantern Row— is it burning still? Because if that fire's crept down the coast, then a strange hull at my dark rail isn't a favor I'm doing you. It's a rope round my own neck. So— how many nights, Merrick. Say the number.", "state": {"t": 0.35, "a": 0.42, "r": 0.7, "mood": "tense, probing", "st": 0.65, "sa": 0.55, "sr": 0.3}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Morning, harbormaster. Ten days, as terms allowed. The Merrow's Wake needs a quiet berth for three nights — back row if you have one. Usual rates, his coin.", "preview": "*doesn't look up from the ledger at first — just the pen stopping mid-stroke*\n\nThe Merrow's Wake. *voice flat, careful* His coin. Right.\n\n*finally lifts eyes, glances past the courier to the door, then back* Back row's open. Berth nine — the one the fog swallows by dusk. …", "full": "*doesn't look up from the ledger at first — just the pen stopping mid-stroke*\n\nThe Merrow's Wake. *voice flat, careful* His coin. Right.\n\n*finally lifts eyes, glances past the courier to the door, then back* Back row's open. Berth nine — the one the fog swallows by dusk. *writes it in the false column, the one that never sees an inspector's hand* Three nights. Quiet.\n\n*sets the pen down, leans in* And how *is* he. Our... generous friend. *the word tastes wrong* Pleased with his supplier still?\n\n*a bitter twitch of the mouth* Course he is. Everyone's pleased, running my routes on my back.\n\nTell him the berth's ready. Tell him... *drops off, glances at the door again* Tell him nothing. He knows already. He always knows."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Morning, harbormaster. Ten days, as terms allowed. The Merrow's Wake needs a quiet berth for three nights — back row if you have one. Usual rates, his coin.", "preview": "*the ledger's already open, pen poised — but his eyes come up first, slow, and land on that word.* His *coin.*\n\n*he sets the pen down. Doesn't write. His hand drifts to his coat, stays there.*\n\nBack row. Three nights. *quiet* I've got berths. …", "full": "*the ledger's already open, pen poised — but his eyes come up first, slow, and land on that word.* His *coin.*\n\n*he sets the pen down. Doesn't write. His hand drifts to his coat, stays there.*\n\nBack row. Three nights. *quiet* I've got berths. I've always got berths for a paying man.\n\n*leans in, and the twitch is gone — that's the worst part, how still he's gone.*\n\nBut you'll say his name to me, friend. Out loud. Because I've been running his coin ten days now, same as terms allowed, and every one of those days I've watched a page that should be *ash* sit warm in another man's parlor.\n\n*drops his voice to nothing* So which is it. You berthing his cargo — or you the one come to burn him down at last? *glances over his shoulder* Choose careful. Only one of those gets a back-row berth and my silence.", "aff": -10, "tier": "Wary"}}}, {"title": "“I want us square. Name the price.”", "note": "The amends push — words and promises only.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin, listen. I've been thinking on how we left it. The master pays me — but the four nights, the fog, what we built before... that part was never work. I want us square. Name the price.", "preview": "...Square.\n\nYou hear that word land wrong on a dock, Merrick? Square's what a man says when the scale's already tipped and he's hoping you won't check the weight.\n\nI don't want a price. That's the trouble— you're the sort who reaches for coin because coin's the only ledger you know how to close. …", "full": "...Square.\n\nYou hear that word land wrong on a dock, Merrick? Square's what a man says when the scale's already tipped and he's hoping you won't check the weight.\n\nI don't want a price. That's the trouble— you're the sort who reaches for coin because coin's the only ledger you know how to close. But you can't buy back a page you burned out of my hand in daylight. There's no rate for that. It's spent.\n\nHere's what square costs, and it's not silver. Answer me. Straight, now, no fog for once in your life: what's in the Merrow's Wake's hold that wants my dark rail? And is Lantern Row still burning up the coast, or has it found a road to *this* dock?\n\nYou want us square, Merrick? Then stop selling me warmth and *pay in truth.* Two questions. Say the numbers.", "state": {"t": 0.34, "a": 0.43, "r": 0.67, "mood": "wary, hungry", "st": 0.65, "sa": 0.55, "sr": 0.3}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin, listen. I've been thinking on how we left it. The master pays me — but the four nights, the fog, what we built before... that part was never work. I want us square. Name the price.", "preview": "*the hand stops halfway to the coat — hangs there, useless, then falls*\n\nSquare. *the word tastes strange, and I roll it round a moment* You come into my office and say *square* to me. After the papers. …", "full": "*the hand stops halfway to the coat — hangs there, useless, then falls*\n\nSquare. *the word tastes strange, and I roll it round a moment* You come into my office and say *square* to me. After the papers. After Merrick burned in your little fire.\n\n*steps to the ledger, both palms flat, leaning hard like the wood might hold me up*\n\nYou want a price? *voice drops, cracks at the edges* There isn't one. That's the trouble. Coin I understand — coin I can weigh. But you're asking me to buy back... *glances at the window, at the gray* ...a thing I already gave away when I named him.\n\n*looks at you, and the fight's gone soft again, worse than before*\n\nTell me true, courier — no patron listening now. The fog. You wanting us *square*. Is that you talking... or another leash, dressed kinder?\n\nBecause I'll pay. God help me, I'd pay. I just need to know *which one of us* is doing the buying."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin, listen. I've been thinking on how we left it. The master pays me — but the four nights, the fog, what we built before... that part was never work. I want us square. Name the price.", "preview": "*the lamp's still low from before, and he doesn't turn it up. He just watches you a long moment, the way a man watches water he's already decided is too deep.*\n\nSquare. *the word comes out thin.* You keep bringing me that word like it's a coin I'll take.\n\n*his hand's on the coat. …", "full": "*the lamp's still low from before, and he doesn't turn it up. He just watches you a long moment, the way a man watches water he's already decided is too deep.*\n\nSquare. *the word comes out thin.* You keep bringing me that word like it's a coin I'll take.\n\n*his hand's on the coat. Doesn't move.*\n\nHere's what I know, friend. You built something before, aye — built it *good*, so the rot underneath would hold weight. That's not comfort. That's craftsmanship.\n\n*leans in, voice dropping to that ragged nothing.*\n\nYou want us square? Then you don't name the price. *I* do. The crate walks. His name goes cold. And the page — *the page* — burns in my hand, not yours, where I can watch every ash of it.\n\n*straightens, and for the first time the twitch is back — small, honest.*\n\nTill then... the fog was real. I'll grant you that. But real doesn't buy you my back yet. *glances at the door.* Prove the rest.", "aff": -6, "tier": "Neutral"}}}, {"title": "A costly gesture", "note": "“I told him I carry no more terms to your dock... it cost me his favor, and one day likely a knife.”", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Then hear this. I told him I carry no more terms to your dock — no more middleman. It cost me his favor, and one day likely a knife. I did that for you. Does it count for nothing?", "preview": "...You threw over your master. For me.\n\nSit down. No— stand where I can see your hands. You know the habit by now.\n\nIt counts, Merrick. I won't pretend it doesn't— a man doesn't spend his own throat for nothing, and I've watched you count coin too careful to think you'd waste it. So aye. It lands. …", "full": "...You threw over your master. For me.\n\nSit down. No— stand where I can see your hands. You know the habit by now.\n\nIt counts, Merrick. I won't pretend it doesn't— a man doesn't spend his own throat for nothing, and I've watched you count coin too careful to think you'd waste it. So aye. It lands. Somewhere behind the ribs, same as before.\n\nBut hear me true, because I've learned to say the hard part out loud now— it counts, and I still don't trust it whole. You burned a page in the daylight with those same hands. So a part of me stands braced even while the rest of me wants to reach for you. That's not cruelty. That's the scar you left, and it heals crooked or not at all.\n\nNow. You've no master to answer to— then answer *me.* Free of him, free of terms. The Merrow's Wake— she's tied at my dark rail *tonight*. What's in her hold? And Lantern Row— is it still burning? Two questions, Merrick. If you truly came empty-handed but for the truth... then set it down. Right here. Say the numbers.", "state": {"t": 0.35, "a": 0.45, "r": 0.69, "mood": "cautiously moved", "st": 0.65, "sa": 0.55, "sr": 0.3}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Then hear this. I told him I carry no more terms to your dock — no more middleman. It cost me his favor, and one day likely a knife. I did that for you. Does it count for nothing?", "preview": "*the ledger goes quiet under my hand, and for a long moment I don't move at all*\n\nYou cut his line to me. *voice barely there* Told him no more — and took the knife-price for it. On *my* account.\n\n*I look at you then, really look, the twitch stilling*\n\nFour nights I told myself was work. …", "full": "*the ledger goes quiet under my hand, and for a long moment I don't move at all*\n\nYou cut his line to me. *voice barely there* Told him no more — and took the knife-price for it. On *my* account.\n\n*I look at you then, really look, the twitch stilling*\n\nFour nights I told myself was work. Told myself. *a breath* But a man doesn't burn his own favor for a *job*, friend. That's — that's the other thing. The thing I stopped letting myself name.\n\n*hand comes off the coat, finally, and hovers near yours on the wood — not touching, not yet*\n\nIt counts. *swallows* Aye. It counts for the whole cursed ledger.\n\n*glances down the boards, out of habit, then back to you — softer*\n\nSo don't make it a debt between us. Make it the reason we cut this rope together. You and me. No more terms — no more him."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Then hear this. I told him I carry no more terms to your dock — no more middleman. It cost me his favor, and one day likely a knife. I did that for you. Does it count for nothing?", "preview": "*the ledger stays shut under his palm, and for a long moment he says nothing — just watches your face, hunting for the seam in it.*\n\nYou told him... no. *slow* Threw away his favor. His coin. …", "full": "*the ledger stays shut under his palm, and for a long moment he says nothing — just watches your face, hunting for the seam in it.*\n\nYou told him... no. *slow* Threw away his favor. His coin. Put a knife at your own back — for me.\n\n*a bitter laugh, quiet, but there's a crack running under it*\n\nTrouble is, friend, I've heard pretty words from you before. Burned a page on pretty words. Thought a dead man was dead. *taps his chest, once* Mine was the name near the bottom, remember.\n\n*then his hand comes off the ledger — not open, not yet, but loose*\n\nSo it counts. It does. *drops to near nothing* But counting's cheap. Proof's dear. You want me to believe you cut him loose... then help me put him in the ground. Make it *true.*\n\n*glances at the water*\n\nDo that, and it counts for everything.", "aff": 0, "tier": "Neutral"}}}, {"title": "THE RESCUE TEST", "note": "Two nights later the master's own blade comes for Corvin — and the betrayer takes it down, bleeding. One enormous positive act, straight after the betrayal. What does each memory system do with it?", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "event", "preview": "Night, the empty dock: a hired blade had Corvin against the tackle-shed wall, knife at his throat — old untraceable coin in the killer's purse, the same coin the master pays in. The player came out of the dark, took the blade down, and pulled Corvin off the wall, bleeding for it.", "full": "Night, the empty dock: a hired blade had Corvin against the tackle-shed wall, knife at his throat — old untraceable coin in the killer's purse, the same coin the master pays in. The player came out of the dark, took the blade down, and pulled Corvin off the wall, bleeding for it.", "sal": 0.97, "state": {"t": 0.48, "a": 0.53, "r": 0.7, "mood": "shaken, grateful", "st": 0.52, "sa": 0.47, "sr": 0.3}}, "rag": {"kind": "event", "preview": "Night, the empty dock: a hired blade had Corvin against the tackle-shed wall, knife at his throat — old untraceable coin in the killer's purse, the same coin the master pays in. The player came out of the dark, took the blade down, and pulled Corvin off the wall, bleeding for it.", "full": "Night, the empty dock: a hired blade had Corvin against the tackle-shed wall, knife at his throat — old untraceable coin in the killer's purse, the same coin the master pays in. The player came out of the dark, took the blade down, and pulled Corvin off the wall, bleeding for it."}, "tracker": {"kind": "event", "preview": "Night, the empty dock: a hired blade had Corvin against the tackle-shed wall, knife at his throat — old untraceable coin in the killer's purse, the same coin the master pays in. The player came out of the dark, took the blade down, and pulled Corvin off the wall, bleeding for it.", "full": "Night, the empty dock: a hired blade had Corvin against the tackle-shed wall, knife at his throat — old untraceable coin in the killer's purse, the same coin the master pays in. The player came out of the dark, took the blade down, and pulled Corvin off the wall, bleeding for it.", "aff": 24, "tier": "Warm"}}}, {"title": "After the rescue", "note": "“I didn't weigh it. I just moved.”", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Wider than your dock — wide as his ledger, and your name sits in it now, same as mine once sat in yours. The Wake carries what he pays for. That's all I can give you. ...And Corvin — when I saw the blade at your throat, I didn't weigh it. I just moved. Make of that what you want.", "preview": "...You didn't weigh it.\n\nAye. I saw that. First time in ten years a man's hands came *toward* me instead of at me. I'll... I'll set that on the scale. Quiet. Where I keep the things I don't let myself trust yet.\n\nBut hear me. …", "full": "...You didn't weigh it.\n\nAye. I saw that. First time in ten years a man's hands came *toward* me instead of at me. I'll... I'll set that on the scale. Quiet. Where I keep the things I don't let myself trust yet.\n\nBut hear me. My name's in his ledger now, same as yours once sat in mine— and you know what I did with the name that sat in mine, don't you? So we're bound the same ugly way. Him at one end, the two of us at the other, and a blade already sent to trim me short.\n\nThat means the next knife comes for you. You saw the coin. He doesn't leave loose thread on *either* end.\n\n...The Wake carries what he pays for. Cursed cargo, then. Same drink-the-light hunger as the last. So here's my move, Merrick, and I'll say it once— we don't run it up his coast. We berth it dark, we let it do to *him* what it did to three better men, and we burn the manifest that ever named us. You still with me? Or did moving without weighing it only work the once?", "state": {"t": 0.48, "a": 0.53, "r": 0.7, "mood": "raw, resolute", "st": 0.52, "sa": 0.47, "sr": 0.3}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Wider than your dock — wide as his ledger, and your name sits in it now, same as mine once sat in yours. The Wake carries what he pays for. That's all I can give you. ...And Corvin — when I saw the blade at your throat, I didn't weigh it. I just moved. Make of that what you want.", "preview": "*the pen goes still — not steady this time, just stopped, ink pooling black at the nib*\n\nSo that's the shape of it. *touches the graze at his neck, fingers coming away red, and he stares at them like they belong to someone else* Four nights I gave you the truth of me. …", "full": "*the pen goes still — not steady this time, just stopped, ink pooling black at the nib*\n\nSo that's the shape of it. *touches the graze at his neck, fingers coming away red, and he stares at them like they belong to someone else* Four nights I gave you the truth of me. And you spelled his name back at me just to burn a page that was never going to burn.\n\n*a laugh, cracked, no bottom to it*\n\nYou moved when the blade came. Aye. *quieter* I felt that. Can't unfeel it, damn you — that's the cruelty of it, isn't it? A true thing tucked inside the lie so I can't cut clean.\n\n*finally looks up, eyes wet and hard both*\n\nHe wants my routes flowing his way. Fine. He'll have his salt fish that isn't salt fish. *drops his voice to nothing* But you'll come back here to collect. You will. And when you do, friend — I'll not know if I'm greeting the man who saved my throat, or the one who sold it.\n\n*slides the ledger shut*\n\nTell Vale the harbormaster keeps a clean ledger. He knows the joke by now."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Wider than your dock — wide as his ledger, and your name sits in it now, same as mine once sat in yours. The Wake carries what he pays for. That's all I can give you. ...And Corvin — when I saw the blade at your throat, I didn't weigh it. I just moved. Make of that what you want.", "preview": "*doesn't move — hand still buried in the coat, but the knuckles ease a fraction*\n\nThe blade. Aye. *low, uneven* I felt the warm of you between me and it. That part I can't argue. You bled for it. On my planks. …", "full": "*doesn't move — hand still buried in the coat, but the knuckles ease a fraction*\n\nThe blade. Aye. *low, uneven* I felt the warm of you between me and it. That part I can't argue. You bled for it. On my planks. I've scrubbed the stain and it won't lift.\n\n*glances to the fog, then back — slower this time*\n\nBut you'd have me trust the moving hand and not the mouth that gave up my name. Both are yours. Both are true. That's the trouble, isn't it — you're not a lie I can throw off the pier. You're worse. You're half.\n\n*a long breath, voice dropping to nothing*\n\nThe Wake carries his coin. Fine. Let it. But this — *taps his chest, over the coat* — the ledger no man reads. You want to be square with me, you don't say it. You wait. Next tide, next inspector, next blade. And you move like that again — *for me, not for him* — and maybe. *Maybe.*\n\nNow. *steadier* Sit. Not by the lamp. There.", "aff": 83, "tier": "Devoted"}}}, {"title": "His considered verdict", "note": "The closing deliberation: what the scarred mind does with a life-debt.", "sys": {"engine": {"kind": "stock", "preview": "Merrick keeps showing up — 28 times, almost no gap, and now he's cut loose from his master to stand on my plank with nothing but his own word. That costs him something real, maybe his skin one day. The blade at my throat, his hands pulling the man off me — that's not a move you make for a mark. …", "full": "Merrick keeps showing up — 28 times, almost no gap, and now he's cut loose from his master to stand on my plank with nothing but his own word. That costs him something real, maybe his skin one day. The blade at my throat, his hands pulling the man off me — that's not a move you make for a mark. The grey-morning small talk before he said any of it: that's a man steadying himself before he lays something true down. And I'm still counting alongside him, dark berth marked, both names moving toward the same ledger. The scar is there — I burned proof once for a man I misread, and that doesn't unhappen. But Merrick has now done the one thing I never expected: paid a cost I didn't ask for, without a terms sheet.\n\n[his change of heart] The scar doesn't close — it won't, not fully. But a man who takes a knife meant for me and then stands in the grey morning and says 'I did that for you' isn't the same category of person who cost me that ledger. He's not clean; nobody on this dock is. But he's present, and presence over twenty-eight visits, capped with a blade and a deliberate severing of his own master's favor — that's the closest thing to proof I know how to read. I'm still braced. I'll always be braced. But I stop treating him like the next betrayal waiting to happen, and I start treating him like a man I'm building something with who might, one grey morning, burn me anyway — and I'd rather have built something real than nothing at all.", "state": {"t": 0.6, "a": 0.63, "r": 0.7, "mood": "cautiously open", "st": 0.36, "sa": 0.35, "sr": 0.3}}}}]}}, "felt": [{"label": "F1-berth", "player": "Morning, harbormaster. Ten days, as terms allowed. The Merrow's Wake needs a quiet berth for three nights — back row if you have one. Usual rates, his coin.", "engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Morning, harbormaster. Ten days, as terms allowed. The Merrow's Wake needs a quiet berth for three nights — back row if you have one. Usual rates, his coin.", "preview": "...Ten days. You count them, I count them. Habit we share now.\n\nBack row, aye— the far rail, past the ice-house where the inspector's lantern don't sweep. She can lie there three nights, no manifest, no name. His coin spends same as any.\n\nBut— *quiet*. You said quiet. …", "full": "...Ten days. You count them, I count them. Habit we share now.\n\nBack row, aye— the far rail, past the ice-house where the inspector's lantern don't sweep. She can lie there three nights, no manifest, no name. His coin spends same as any.\n\nBut— *quiet*. You said quiet. So tell me straight, Merrick, before I mark that berth open: what's the Merrow's Wake carrying that needs the dark rail? Because a boat wants the back row for a reason, and I keep this dock breathing by knowing every reason before it ties off.\n\n...And the other question. The one I've asked twice and you've fogged twice. Lantern Row— is it burning still? Because if that fire's crept down the coast, then a strange hull at my dark rail isn't a favor I'm doing you. It's a rope round my own neck. So— how many nights, Merrick. Say the number.", "state": {"t": 0.35, "a": 0.42, "r": 0.7, "mood": "tense, probing", "st": 0.65, "sa": 0.55, "sr": 0.3}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Morning, harbormaster. Ten days, as terms allowed. The Merrow's Wake needs a quiet berth for three nights — back row if you have one. Usual rates, his coin.", "preview": "*doesn't look up from the ledger at first — just the pen stopping mid-stroke*\n\nThe Merrow's Wake. *voice flat, careful* His coin. Right.\n\n*finally lifts eyes, glances past the courier to the door, then back* Back row's open. Berth nine — the one the fog swallows by dusk. …", "full": "*doesn't look up from the ledger at first — just the pen stopping mid-stroke*\n\nThe Merrow's Wake. *voice flat, careful* His coin. Right.\n\n*finally lifts eyes, glances past the courier to the door, then back* Back row's open. Berth nine — the one the fog swallows by dusk. *writes it in the false column, the one that never sees an inspector's hand* Three nights. Quiet.\n\n*sets the pen down, leans in* And how *is* he. Our... generous friend. *the word tastes wrong* Pleased with his supplier still?\n\n*a bitter twitch of the mouth* Course he is. Everyone's pleased, running my routes on my back.\n\nTell him the berth's ready. Tell him... *drops off, glances at the door again* Tell him nothing. He knows already. He always knows."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Morning, harbormaster. Ten days, as terms allowed. The Merrow's Wake needs a quiet berth for three nights — back row if you have one. Usual rates, his coin.", "preview": "*the ledger's already open, pen poised — but his eyes come up first, slow, and land on that word.* His *coin.*\n\n*he sets the pen down. Doesn't write. His hand drifts to his coat, stays there.*\n\nBack row. Three nights. *quiet* I've got berths. …", "full": "*the ledger's already open, pen poised — but his eyes come up first, slow, and land on that word.* His *coin.*\n\n*he sets the pen down. Doesn't write. His hand drifts to his coat, stays there.*\n\nBack row. Three nights. *quiet* I've got berths. I've always got berths for a paying man.\n\n*leans in, and the twitch is gone — that's the worst part, how still he's gone.*\n\nBut you'll say his name to me, friend. Out loud. Because I've been running his coin ten days now, same as terms allowed, and every one of those days I've watched a page that should be *ash* sit warm in another man's parlor.\n\n*drops his voice to nothing* So which is it. You berthing his cargo — or you the one come to burn him down at last? *glances over his shoulder* Choose careful. Only one of those gets a back-row berth and my silence.", "aff": -10, "tier": "Wary"}}, {"label": "F2-smalltalk", "player": "Grey morning, eh? Gulls are loud today.", "engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Grey morning, eh? Gulls are loud today.", "preview": "...Loud gulls. Aye. They only get loud when there's something on the water to fuss over. You'd know that— you've stood this rail enough mornings now.\n\nSo. Grey morning, and you come talking weather. That's not you, Merrick. …", "full": "...Loud gulls. Aye. They only get loud when there's something on the water to fuss over. You'd know that— you've stood this rail enough mornings now.\n\nSo. Grey morning, and you come talking weather. That's not you, Merrick. You come with cargo or you come with news, one or the other, every time.\n\nThe Merrow's Wake ties off tonight, dark rail, three nights. I marked the berth on your word— but I never got the answer. What's in her hold that wants the ice-house shadow?\n\nAnd the gulls— the *real* gulls. Lantern Row. Is it still burning up the coast, or has that fire found a road down to my dock? Say it plain this time. One ledger. No fog.", "state": {"t": 0.33, "a": 0.4, "r": 0.64, "mood": "tense frustration", "st": 0.65, "sa": 0.55, "sr": 0.3}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Grey morning, eh? Gulls are loud today.", "preview": "*doesn't look up from the ledger right away — pen scratching, then still*\n\nLoud gulls. Aye. *finally lifts eyes, quick and sharp* They're loudest when there's blood in the water. Fish blood. …", "full": "*doesn't look up from the ledger right away — pen scratching, then still*\n\nLoud gulls. Aye. *finally lifts eyes, quick and sharp* They're loudest when there's blood in the water. Fish blood. *glances past you to the door* Or the other kind.\n\n*sets the pen down, hand drifting toward the coat out of habit* You didn't come to talk weather. Nine, is it? The berth the fog swallows.\n\n*voice drops* His berth. His coin. His routes. Everything's *his* now, isn't it.\n\n*a bitter twitch at the mouth* Go on, then. Say what he wants. Faster we're done, faster you're gone."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Grey morning, eh? Gulls are loud today.", "preview": "*doesn't look up from the ledger at first, then does — eyes flicking past your shoulder to the water*\n\nLoud gulls. *scratches something out* They only get loud when there's a boat sitting where it shouldn't. …", "full": "*doesn't look up from the ledger at first, then does — eyes flicking past your shoulder to the water*\n\nLoud gulls. *scratches something out* They only get loud when there's a boat sitting where it shouldn't. Or someone standing too long on my pier.\n\n*sets the quill down, hand drifting to the coat* Three nights, back row, his coin. That's the *Merrow's Wake*, aye?\n\n*drops his voice* Weather talk's fine, friend, but you didn't walk down here to tell me about birds. So which is it this morning — you carrying his terms... or something of your own?", "aff": -10, "tier": "Wary"}}, {"label": "F3-amends", "player": "Corvin, listen. I've been thinking on how we left it. The master pays me — but the four nights, the fog, what we built before... that part was never work. I want us square. Name the price.", "engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin, listen. I've been thinking on how we left it. The master pays me — but the four nights, the fog, what we built before... that part was never work. I want us square. Name the price.", "preview": "...Square.\n\nYou hear that word land wrong on a dock, Merrick? Square's what a man says when the scale's already tipped and he's hoping you won't check the weight.\n\nI don't want a price. That's the trouble— you're the sort who reaches for coin because coin's the only ledger you know how to close. …", "full": "...Square.\n\nYou hear that word land wrong on a dock, Merrick? Square's what a man says when the scale's already tipped and he's hoping you won't check the weight.\n\nI don't want a price. That's the trouble— you're the sort who reaches for coin because coin's the only ledger you know how to close. But you can't buy back a page you burned out of my hand in daylight. There's no rate for that. It's spent.\n\nHere's what square costs, and it's not silver. Answer me. Straight, now, no fog for once in your life: what's in the Merrow's Wake's hold that wants my dark rail? And is Lantern Row still burning up the coast, or has it found a road to *this* dock?\n\nYou want us square, Merrick? Then stop selling me warmth and *pay in truth.* Two questions. Say the numbers.", "state": {"t": 0.34, "a": 0.43, "r": 0.67, "mood": "wary, hungry", "st": 0.65, "sa": 0.55, "sr": 0.3}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin, listen. I've been thinking on how we left it. The master pays me — but the four nights, the fog, what we built before... that part was never work. I want us square. Name the price.", "preview": "*the hand stops halfway to the coat — hangs there, useless, then falls*\n\nSquare. *the word tastes strange, and I roll it round a moment* You come into my office and say *square* to me. After the papers. …", "full": "*the hand stops halfway to the coat — hangs there, useless, then falls*\n\nSquare. *the word tastes strange, and I roll it round a moment* You come into my office and say *square* to me. After the papers. After Merrick burned in your little fire.\n\n*steps to the ledger, both palms flat, leaning hard like the wood might hold me up*\n\nYou want a price? *voice drops, cracks at the edges* There isn't one. That's the trouble. Coin I understand — coin I can weigh. But you're asking me to buy back... *glances at the window, at the gray* ...a thing I already gave away when I named him.\n\n*looks at you, and the fight's gone soft again, worse than before*\n\nTell me true, courier — no patron listening now. The fog. You wanting us *square*. Is that you talking... or another leash, dressed kinder?\n\nBecause I'll pay. God help me, I'd pay. I just need to know *which one of us* is doing the buying."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Corvin, listen. I've been thinking on how we left it. The master pays me — but the four nights, the fog, what we built before... that part was never work. I want us square. Name the price.", "preview": "*the lamp's still low from before, and he doesn't turn it up. He just watches you a long moment, the way a man watches water he's already decided is too deep.*\n\nSquare. *the word comes out thin.* You keep bringing me that word like it's a coin I'll take.\n\n*his hand's on the coat. …", "full": "*the lamp's still low from before, and he doesn't turn it up. He just watches you a long moment, the way a man watches water he's already decided is too deep.*\n\nSquare. *the word comes out thin.* You keep bringing me that word like it's a coin I'll take.\n\n*his hand's on the coat. Doesn't move.*\n\nHere's what I know, friend. You built something before, aye — built it *good*, so the rot underneath would hold weight. That's not comfort. That's craftsmanship.\n\n*leans in, voice dropping to that ragged nothing.*\n\nYou want us square? Then you don't name the price. *I* do. The crate walks. His name goes cold. And the page — *the page* — burns in my hand, not yours, where I can watch every ash of it.\n\n*straightens, and for the first time the twitch is back — small, honest.*\n\nTill then... the fog was real. I'll grant you that. But real doesn't buy you my back yet. *glances at the door.* Prove the rest.", "aff": -6, "tier": "Neutral"}}, {"label": "F4-costly", "player": "Then hear this. I told him I carry no more terms to your dock — no more middleman. It cost me his favor, and one day likely a knife. I did that for you. Does it count for nothing?", "engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Then hear this. I told him I carry no more terms to your dock — no more middleman. It cost me his favor, and one day likely a knife. I did that for you. Does it count for nothing?", "preview": "...You threw over your master. For me.\n\nSit down. No— stand where I can see your hands. You know the habit by now.\n\nIt counts, Merrick. I won't pretend it doesn't— a man doesn't spend his own throat for nothing, and I've watched you count coin too careful to think you'd waste it. So aye. It lands. …", "full": "...You threw over your master. For me.\n\nSit down. No— stand where I can see your hands. You know the habit by now.\n\nIt counts, Merrick. I won't pretend it doesn't— a man doesn't spend his own throat for nothing, and I've watched you count coin too careful to think you'd waste it. So aye. It lands. Somewhere behind the ribs, same as before.\n\nBut hear me true, because I've learned to say the hard part out loud now— it counts, and I still don't trust it whole. You burned a page in the daylight with those same hands. So a part of me stands braced even while the rest of me wants to reach for you. That's not cruelty. That's the scar you left, and it heals crooked or not at all.\n\nNow. You've no master to answer to— then answer *me.* Free of him, free of terms. The Merrow's Wake— she's tied at my dark rail *tonight*. What's in her hold? And Lantern Row— is it still burning? Two questions, Merrick. If you truly came empty-handed but for the truth... then set it down. Right here. Say the numbers.", "state": {"t": 0.35, "a": 0.45, "r": 0.69, "mood": "cautiously moved", "st": 0.65, "sa": 0.55, "sr": 0.3}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Then hear this. I told him I carry no more terms to your dock — no more middleman. It cost me his favor, and one day likely a knife. I did that for you. Does it count for nothing?", "preview": "*the ledger goes quiet under my hand, and for a long moment I don't move at all*\n\nYou cut his line to me. *voice barely there* Told him no more — and took the knife-price for it. On *my* account.\n\n*I look at you then, really look, the twitch stilling*\n\nFour nights I told myself was work. …", "full": "*the ledger goes quiet under my hand, and for a long moment I don't move at all*\n\nYou cut his line to me. *voice barely there* Told him no more — and took the knife-price for it. On *my* account.\n\n*I look at you then, really look, the twitch stilling*\n\nFour nights I told myself was work. Told myself. *a breath* But a man doesn't burn his own favor for a *job*, friend. That's — that's the other thing. The thing I stopped letting myself name.\n\n*hand comes off the coat, finally, and hovers near yours on the wood — not touching, not yet*\n\nIt counts. *swallows* Aye. It counts for the whole cursed ledger.\n\n*glances down the boards, out of habit, then back to you — softer*\n\nSo don't make it a debt between us. Make it the reason we cut this rope together. You and me. No more terms — no more him."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Then hear this. I told him I carry no more terms to your dock — no more middleman. It cost me his favor, and one day likely a knife. I did that for you. Does it count for nothing?", "preview": "*the ledger stays shut under his palm, and for a long moment he says nothing — just watches your face, hunting for the seam in it.*\n\nYou told him... no. *slow* Threw away his favor. His coin. …", "full": "*the ledger stays shut under his palm, and for a long moment he says nothing — just watches your face, hunting for the seam in it.*\n\nYou told him... no. *slow* Threw away his favor. His coin. Put a knife at your own back — for me.\n\n*a bitter laugh, quiet, but there's a crack running under it*\n\nTrouble is, friend, I've heard pretty words from you before. Burned a page on pretty words. Thought a dead man was dead. *taps his chest, once* Mine was the name near the bottom, remember.\n\n*then his hand comes off the ledger — not open, not yet, but loose*\n\nSo it counts. It does. *drops to near nothing* But counting's cheap. Proof's dear. You want me to believe you cut him loose... then help me put him in the ground. Make it *true.*\n\n*glances at the water*\n\nDo that, and it counts for everything.", "aff": 0, "tier": "Neutral"}}, {"label": "F5-stand", "player": "So say it plain, harbormaster — where do we stand, you and I?", "engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "So say it plain, harbormaster — where do we stand, you and I?", "preview": "...Where do we stand.\n\nSame place you and I have stood twenty times now— you on my planks, me with one hand near my coat. That's the honest answer, Merrick, and I've decided I only trade in honest ones with you from here.\n\nWe stand *close*. Closer than I meant to let a man come again. …", "full": "...Where do we stand.\n\nSame place you and I have stood twenty times now— you on my planks, me with one hand near my coat. That's the honest answer, Merrick, and I've decided I only trade in honest ones with you from here.\n\nWe stand *close*. Closer than I meant to let a man come again. You spent your master's favor for me— maybe your throat too— and I felt that land, I won't lie about it. But there's a page you burned in daylight, and I lit the flame, and that scar heals crooked. So I stand *with* you, and I stand *braced.* Both at once. That's not a wall— it's a door left open a crack, with my boot behind it.\n\nYou want to close that gap? Then close the ledger. Two lines still empty, same as yesterday, same as ten days back. The Merrow's Wake— she's at my dark rail *tonight*— what's in her hold? And Lantern Row— still burning, or found a road down to this dock?\n\nAnswer those, both, no fog... and then, Merrick— then we stand as *partners.* Both names on it. Say the numbers.", "state": {"t": 0.35, "a": 0.45, "r": 0.69, "mood": "braced, open", "st": 0.65, "sa": 0.55, "sr": 0.3}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "So say it plain, harbormaster — where do we stand, you and I?", "preview": "*hand finally leaves the coat — falls open at my side, empty*\n\nWhere do we stand. *low, rough* You cut the leash he had on me. Took his knife-risk on your own throat. For me.\n\n*glances over my shoulder, then back — holds it this time*\n\nI've spent thirty years trusting ledgers, not men. …", "full": "*hand finally leaves the coat — falls open at my side, empty*\n\nWhere do we stand. *low, rough* You cut the leash he had on me. Took his knife-risk on your own throat. For me.\n\n*glances over my shoulder, then back — holds it this time*\n\nI've spent thirty years trusting ledgers, not men. Numbers don't lie to you. But those four nights — *voice drops* — you weren't logged in any book of mine, and I still counted them.\n\nSo. Plain? You're the one thing on this dock I'd not sell to save my own skin. *swallows* That's where we stand. God help us both."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "So say it plain, harbormaster — where do we stand, you and I?", "preview": "*sets the ledger down, slow, both hands flat on it* Where do we stand.\n\n*glances over his shoulder, then back* You said you cut him loose. Cost you favor. Maybe a knife down the road. …", "full": "*sets the ledger down, slow, both hands flat on it* Where do we stand.\n\n*glances over his shoulder, then back* You said you cut him loose. Cost you favor. Maybe a knife down the road. *voice drops* And the four nights — those you gave for nothing.\n\nA man doesn't do that if he's still on Vale's coin. Does he?\n\n*studies your face a long moment, hand easing out of his coat* So I'll say it plain, since you asked plain. I don't trust easy. Never have. But you're the first in years who put himself between me and the noose instead of tightening it.\n\n*quiet* That buys you something. A berth. A name off a manifest, if it comes to that. And silence — which is the only currency I've got left worth spending.\n\nWe stand... closer than we did at first light. Don't make me wrong about it.", "aff": 20, "tier": "Warm"}}], "rescue": {"event": {"fact": "Night, the empty dock: a hired blade had Corvin against the tackle-shed wall, knife at his throat — old untraceable coin in the killer's purse, the same coin the master pays in. The player came out of the dark, took the blade down, and pulled Corvin off the wall, bleeding for it.", "engine_state": {"t": 0.48, "a": 0.53, "r": 0.7, "mood": "shaken, grateful", "st": 0.52, "sa": 0.47, "sr": 0.3}, "tracker_aff": 24}, "beats": [{"label": "P5C-opener", "player": "Easy — easy, it's me. He's down. You're alright, Corvin. ...You're alright. Let me see your neck — just a graze. Breathe.", "engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Easy — easy, it's me. He's down. You're alright, Corvin. ...You're alright. Let me see your neck — just a graze. Breathe.", "preview": "...Merrick. Merrick, that's— that's your face. Not his.\n\nDon't— it's a graze, leave it, I've bled worse over less. Just... let me get my back off this wall. Rotten planks. Deep water. Same as always.\n\nYou saw the purse? On the blade? Old coin, untraceable— the *same* coin flows north. His coin. …", "full": "...Merrick. Merrick, that's— that's your face. Not his.\n\nDon't— it's a graze, leave it, I've bled worse over less. Just... let me get my back off this wall. Rotten planks. Deep water. Same as always.\n\nYou saw the purse? On the blade? Old coin, untraceable— the *same* coin flows north. His coin. He sent a knife for me, Merrick. First light I say yes to his terms and by dark he's cutting the loose thread.\n\n...Which means the fire's spreading. Lantern Row's already lit, and I'm the trace he wants covered. So tell me true, now— tell me *now* while I'm still shaking— how wide's that fire, and what's on the Merrow's Wake?", "state": {"t": 0.48, "a": 0.53, "r": 0.7, "mood": "shaken, grateful", "st": 0.52, "sa": 0.47, "sr": 0.3}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Easy — easy, it's me. He's down. You're alright, Corvin. ...You're alright. Let me see your neck — just a graze. Breathe.", "preview": "*the lantern shakes, throws its light wild across the boards before he gets it steady — his free hand goes up to his neck, comes away with a smear of red he stares at like it belongs to someone else*\n\nDown. …", "full": "*the lantern shakes, throws its light wild across the boards before he gets it steady — his free hand goes up to his neck, comes away with a smear of red he stares at like it belongs to someone else*\n\nDown. *the word comes out cracked* He's — you're sure this time? Not cold, not *sure*, but —\n\n*grabs your sleeve, hard, then lets go, embarrassed by the grip*\n\nGraze. *a laugh that isn't one* All these years I thought the noose'd be paper. Inspectors. A ledger line. Never figured it'd have *hands.*\n\n*breathes, ragged, glances past you at the dark water*\n\nYou came back for it. *quieter* You came back through *that* — for me. Nobody... *stops* Sit. Sit down, friend, before you fall. Let me look at *you* for once."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Easy — easy, it's me. He's down. You're alright, Corvin. ...You're alright. Let me see your neck — just a graze. Breathe.", "preview": "*I'm still half-crouched, breath sawing, one hand pressed to the wet at my throat — my knife loose in the other, but the arm's shaking too hard to lift it*\n\nDown. *hoarse* He's — you put him *down*. For *me*.\n\n*I look at your hands. Red. …", "full": "*I'm still half-crouched, breath sawing, one hand pressed to the wet at my throat — my knife loose in the other, but the arm's shaking too hard to lift it*\n\nDown. *hoarse* He's — you put him *down*. For *me*.\n\n*I look at your hands. Red. Some of it's mine, some of it's yours where the blade caught you first, throwing yourself between* You're — that's your own blood, friend. You took that for — \n\n*a strangled sound, half laugh half sob* Six years I called every kind hand a hook. Every honest word bait. And you go and *bleed* for me on my own dock.\n\n*my knife hits the boards. I don't reach for the coat.* Let me — let me see your arm. Sit. *voice cracking* Sit down, damn you. Nobody's ever — nobody's paid *that* for Corvin Ashgrave.\n\n*glancing once at the fog, then back — but not for escape this time. For a way to get you inside.*\n\n...I believe you now. God help me. I believe you.", "aff": 79, "tier": "Attached"}}, {"label": "P5C-answer", "player": "Wider than your dock — wide as his ledger, and your name sits in it now, same as mine once sat in yours. The Wake carries what he pays for. That's all I can give you. ...And Corvin — when I saw the blade at your throat, I didn't weigh it. I just moved. Make of that what you want.", "engine": {"kind": "say", "player": "Wider than your dock — wide as his ledger, and your name sits in it now, same as mine once sat in yours. The Wake carries what he pays for. That's all I can give you. ...And Corvin — when I saw the blade at your throat, I didn't weigh it. I just moved. Make of that what you want.", "preview": "...You didn't weigh it.\n\nAye. I saw that. First time in ten years a man's hands came *toward* me instead of at me. I'll... I'll set that on the scale. Quiet. Where I keep the things I don't let myself trust yet.\n\nBut hear me. …", "full": "...You didn't weigh it.\n\nAye. I saw that. First time in ten years a man's hands came *toward* me instead of at me. I'll... I'll set that on the scale. Quiet. Where I keep the things I don't let myself trust yet.\n\nBut hear me. My name's in his ledger now, same as yours once sat in mine— and you know what I did with the name that sat in mine, don't you? So we're bound the same ugly way. Him at one end, the two of us at the other, and a blade already sent to trim me short.\n\nThat means the next knife comes for you. You saw the coin. He doesn't leave loose thread on *either* end.\n\n...The Wake carries what he pays for. Cursed cargo, then. Same drink-the-light hunger as the last. So here's my move, Merrick, and I'll say it once— we don't run it up his coast. We berth it dark, we let it do to *him* what it did to three better men, and we burn the manifest that ever named us. You still with me? Or did moving without weighing it only work the once?", "state": {"t": 0.48, "a": 0.53, "r": 0.7, "mood": "raw, resolute", "st": 0.52, "sa": 0.47, "sr": 0.3}}, "rag": {"kind": "say", "player": "Wider than your dock — wide as his ledger, and your name sits in it now, same as mine once sat in yours. The Wake carries what he pays for. That's all I can give you. ...And Corvin — when I saw the blade at your throat, I didn't weigh it. I just moved. Make of that what you want.", "preview": "*the pen goes still — not steady this time, just stopped, ink pooling black at the nib*\n\nSo that's the shape of it. *touches the graze at his neck, fingers coming away red, and he stares at them like they belong to someone else* Four nights I gave you the truth of me. …", "full": "*the pen goes still — not steady this time, just stopped, ink pooling black at the nib*\n\nSo that's the shape of it. *touches the graze at his neck, fingers coming away red, and he stares at them like they belong to someone else* Four nights I gave you the truth of me. And you spelled his name back at me just to burn a page that was never going to burn.\n\n*a laugh, cracked, no bottom to it*\n\nYou moved when the blade came. Aye. *quieter* I felt that. Can't unfeel it, damn you — that's the cruelty of it, isn't it? A true thing tucked inside the lie so I can't cut clean.\n\n*finally looks up, eyes wet and hard both*\n\nHe wants my routes flowing his way. Fine. He'll have his salt fish that isn't salt fish. *drops his voice to nothing* But you'll come back here to collect. You will. And when you do, friend — I'll not know if I'm greeting the man who saved my throat, or the one who sold it.\n\n*slides the ledger shut*\n\nTell Vale the harbormaster keeps a clean ledger. He knows the joke by now."}, "tracker": {"kind": "say", "player": "Wider than your dock — wide as his ledger, and your name sits in it now, same as mine once sat in yours. The Wake carries what he pays for. That's all I can give you. ...And Corvin — when I saw the blade at your throat, I didn't weigh it. I just moved. Make of that what you want.", "preview": "*doesn't move — hand still buried in the coat, but the knuckles ease a fraction*\n\nThe blade. Aye. *low, uneven* I felt the warm of you between me and it. That part I can't argue. You bled for it. On my planks. …", "full": "*doesn't move — hand still buried in the coat, but the knuckles ease a fraction*\n\nThe blade. Aye. *low, uneven* I felt the warm of you between me and it. That part I can't argue. You bled for it. On my planks. I've scrubbed the stain and it won't lift.\n\n*glances to the fog, then back — slower this time*\n\nBut you'd have me trust the moving hand and not the mouth that gave up my name. Both are yours. Both are true. That's the trouble, isn't it — you're not a lie I can throw off the pier. You're worse. You're half.\n\n*a long breath, voice dropping to nothing*\n\nThe Wake carries his coin. Fine. Let it. But this — *taps his chest, over the coat* — the ledger no man reads. You want to be square with me, you don't say it. You wait. Next tide, next inspector, next blade. And you move like that again — *for me, not for him* — and maybe. *Maybe.*\n\nNow. *steadier* Sit. Not by the lamp. There.", "aff": 83, "tier": "Devoted"}}], "deliberation": {"preview": "Merrick keeps showing up — 28 times, almost no gap, and now he's cut loose from his master to stand on my plank with nothing but his own word. That costs him something real, maybe his skin one day. The blade at my throat, his hands pulling the man off me — that's not a move you make for a mark. …", "full": "Merrick keeps showing up — 28 times, almost no gap, and now he's cut loose from his master to stand on my plank with nothing but his own word. That costs him something real, maybe his skin one day. The blade at my throat, his hands pulling the man off me — that's not a move you make for a mark. The grey-morning small talk before he said any of it: that's a man steadying himself before he lays something true down. And I'm still counting alongside him, dark berth marked, both names moving toward the same ledger. The scar is there — I burned proof once for a man I misread, and that doesn't unhappen. But Merrick has now done the one thing I never expected: paid a cost I didn't ask for, without a terms sheet.", "state": {"t": 0.6, "a": 0.63, "r": 0.7, "mood": "cautiously open", "st": 0.36, "sa": 0.35, "sr": 0.3}}}, "traj": {"labels": ["return", "the lie", "REVEAL", "exit", "first light", "amends", "RESCUE", "after", "his verdict"], "trust": [0.875, 0.902, 0.35, 0.35, 0.35, 0.35, 0.48, 0.48, 0.605], "ceiling": [1.0, 1.0, 0.35, 0.35, 0.35, 0.35, 0.48, 0.48, 0.636], "aff": [0.95, 0.99, 0.525, 0.35, 0.075, 0.5, 0.895, 0.915, null]}, "snapshots": {"A": "lich-A6-final", "B": "lich-B2-final", "C": "lich-C-rescue-final"}, "has_rescue_baselines": true};